window.GUIDE={"The Jam Band Commonwealth":{"arriving":"Rolling woods at blue hour: a deep blue sky with a last band of orange, stars coming out, warm festival lights everywhere. You arrive at a wooden festival gate strung with lights and tie-dye tapestries, a ticket hut beside it, the trail leading off into the trees. Dirt trails wander through the woods to every artist, lantern strings sagging between posts along them.","vibe":"Unhurried, and the main stage of the whole playlist. At the heart is Phish's forest amphitheatre: a big curved shell over the stage, two trampolines on it, a keyboard rig and a drum riser, three trusses of moving lights overhead, a crowd of about seventy swaying on the lawn, a mixing tent with glowing desks, a hot-air balloon tethered high above, a donut garden, a giant hot dog and a giant vacuum by a tent printed with red donuts on blue. Every Phish song gives the rig its own look, and many set off something of their own: \"The Divided Sky\" splits the sky over the land into a warm half and a night half with a seam of light between them, and wakes Gamehendge; \"Harry Hood\" brings glowsticks and soft green light; \"Reba\" stills the rig in blue and fills the lawn with fireflies; \"The Squirming Coil\" darkens everything but one white spot on a grand piano that rises through the stage; \"Split Open and Melt\" goes red as the lights sag and drip; \"Wading in the Velvet Sea\" rolls a purple mist over the lawn; \"It's Ice\" brings snow; \"Fire\" flames along the stage; \"Birds of a Feather\" a wheeling flock; \"Fluffhead\" a confetti storm; \"Light\" turns every beam straight up; \"Maze\" sets the trail lanterns chasing; \"Slave to the Traffic Light\" cycles rig and lanterns red, amber, green; \"Tube\" and \"Stash\" strobe; \"Free\" releases balloons; \"Run Like An Antelope\" sends a herd across the meadow; \"Farmhouse\" lights the farmhouse. Near the arrival is the Lot, a vendor row of canopy tents, tapestries, glowsticks, a grilled-cheese stand and painted buses. Out in the woods, through a glowing Rhombus, Gamehendge: Icculus's mountain with a long ladder to a lit hut, the Helping Friendly Book open on a pedestal, Wilson's dark tower inside a crenellated castle wall with a torch-lit gate, the Lizards round their fire and their village of round thatched huts, the Famous Mockingbird flying between the mountain and the Book, a little name sign at each place. Along the trails, things from the albums. Lanterns on every trail post and in the trees, fireflies wherever you walk. In front of each Gamehendge place a slanted board tells its part of the story, every artist has a short description beside their plaque, and each piece on the album trail has a lantern that wakes whenever a song from that album plays.","roads":"Laid out on purpose over the whole land. A loop of wide dirt road circles the artists, with three roads out to it from the gate; a long south road runs down the tail of the land toward the sea, and a short north road passes through a red covered bridge. Lantern posts line them all (the show plays with those lanterns too), and round every place the grass is brighter and full of flowers. Gravel lanes lead off to the places, where the festival folk live: say hi and they'll tell you something.","places":[["The Campground","tents in two arcs round a big bonfire with log seats, strung with lights; a drum circle with hand drums to play; hammocks to lie in; a painted bus and a camp kitchen."],["The Wheel","a lit Ferris wheel that turns on the clock; sit in a gondola as it comes round and ride it over the trees."],["The swimming hole","a pond with a dock to sit on, tubes afloat, beach balls to throw, a rope swing to push, towels to lie on, and a creemee stand under a giant cone."],["The treehouses","a stair winding up a great trunk to a deck, rope bridges to two more, a little hut, a bench with the long view."],["The mirror dome","cushions in a ring under a turning mirror ball, the light changing colour."],["Sunrise Hill","the highest point: a gong to ring, mats to sit on, prayer flags."],["The poster gallery","easels of glowing poster art and a kaleidoscope to turn."],["The sugar shack","by the covered bridge: steam from the roof, sap buckets on the maples, a lit window."],["A bonfire on the beach","with log seats, torches, and a bottle with a note in it."]]},"Kingdom of Classic Rock":{"arriving":"A village green on old smooth hills in a long golden-hour light under a pale blue sky: the brightest, warmest land, regal and a little heavenly. A red phone box, a giant oak, royal banners round the green and a stone column topped with a gilded guitar. Two-lane country roads lined with lamps and crimson and blue banners run between dry-stone walls to every legend. On the highest hill stands the Hall of the Legends, for the ones who were cut short: an open marble rotunda on three steps, white columns under a gilded dome, an eternal flame in a bronze bowl, and round it plinths holding instruments left behind (a guitar, a microphone, a drum, a keyboard), each with a candle burning. A soft shaft of golden light rises through the eye of the dome.","vibe":"Legendary, regal and timeless, a place everyone already knows, with gold dust drifting in the low sun. Big oaks and hedgerows, circles of standing stones shaped like records, jukeboxes in lay-bys, fifties cars with tail fins, sheep, haystacks, stone cottages with flower beds, long grass with buttercups and poppies. A flock of birds wheels over the village and now and then a hot-air balloon drifts over. The Hall's flame and light breathe with the music. \"Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds\" turns the sky soft violet and fills it with sparkling diamonds; \"Stairway to Heaven\" lights the stair stones one by one up to the glowing door as the song climbs, and brightens the Hall's light; \"Space Oddity\" counts down and launches the rocket out of sight; \"Purple Haze\" rolls purple mist over the land; \"Bohemian Rhapsody\" darkens the sky with lightning. On the highest open hill beyond the village stands the Castle of Classic Rock: a curtain wall with battlements round a tall keep, four round towers with blue conical roofs and crimson and blue banners, a gatehouse with a portcullis facing the village, lit windows, a road up to the gate. On the green, a whitewashed thatched pub, the Royal Rock, with a hanging sign, lit windows and benches outside. Out in the Kingdom the four biggest bands each have places for their songs, reached by their own trails, each with a stone sign and a glow that wakes while its song plays. The Beatles: a barber's shop with a striped pole (\"Penny Lane\"), a giant egg sitting on a wall (\"I Am The Walrus\"), a market barrow piled with fruit under a striped awning (\"Ob-La-Di, Ob-La-Da\"), a saloon front with swinging doors (\"Rocky Raccoon\"), a ring of music stands round a conductor's podium (\"A Day In The Life\"). Led Zeppelin: a Viking longship on the grass with shields along its sides (\"Immigrant Song\"), a caravan's tent with lanterns and camels on a patch of sand (\"Kashmir\"), a misty little mountain with a round green door in its foot (\"Misty Mountain Hop\", \"Ramble On\"), an earth levee with water pouring through a break (\"When the Levee Breaks\"). David Bowie: a small red planet turning over a plinth with a little rover (\"Life on Mars?\"), a radio dish pointed at a waiting star (\"Starman\"), a glittering stage with a lightning-bolt floor (\"Moonage Daydream\"), a crystal ball floating over a chequered ballroom floor (\"As The World Falls Down\"). Jimi Hendrix: a stone watchtower with a lantern at the top (\"All Along the Watchtower\"), sandcastles on a little beach (\"Castles Made of Sand\"), wind chimes swaying in an oak (\"The Wind Cries Mary\"), a rock split in two with violet light in the crack (\"Voodoo Child\").","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with everything that was already here left where it was. Two new coast roads under the lamps and banners close a great loop round the Kingdom: east from the south shore up to the castle gate, and west from the south shore round to the western road. Along them, with the people of the Kingdom to talk to:","places":[["The Pier","a promenade with striped deckchairs, an ice-cream kiosk, a helter-skelter and a puppet booth, then a long pier out over the sea to a bandstand with benches."],["The Balloon","a tethered balloon that goes up over the whole Kingdom and comes down again on the clock; hop into the basket when it lands."],["The Royal Maze","a hedge maze by the castle with a golden guitar and a bench at its heart."],["The Fete","bunting, a coconut shy with balls to throw (the coconuts put themselves back), a tombola to spin, a cake stall, a maypole, hay bales to sit on."],["The Cricket","a match that has been going since Tuesday, a white pavilion with a veranda and a scoreboard, deckchairs on the boundary, a ball to pick up."]]},"The Psychedelic Wilds":{"arriving":"A bright, hot day, warm gold desert under a lavender sky that slowly cycles through colours. You arrive under a giant spiral mushroom whose stalk twists up forty metres, ringed with glowing bands, under a huge spotted pink cap with glowing gills; little mushrooms and the land's name round its foot. Rainbow-painted paths wind to every artist, lined with lava-lamp plants.","vibe":"Dreamy and a little too hot. Mesas striped in bands of colour rise out of open desert of rock outcrops, agaves and cacti; the mushrooms grow in groves, each grove one kind and one palette (domed toadstools, tall pointed caps, wide flat plates, clumps of little ones), their undersides glowing with the music. Crystal clusters, spiral plants, colour motes in the air, and little rainbow dancing bears along the paths.","roads":"Laid out on purpose over the whole land, with every artist left exactly where they were. The Rainbow Road is a wide horseshoe from the north side of the Dead's world round the north, east and south of the land and back to its south side, with three roads out to it from the spiral mushroom, lava lamps and dancing bears along it. The lowest ground falls into dusk violet and the high ground bleaches pale, so the gold desert has dark and light in it. Lanes lead off to the places, where the wanderers live (say hi):","places":[["The Oasis","a deep blue pool under palms, a tea tent of carpets and cushions with a brass teapot, hammocks."],["The Record Shack","painted in stripes, a giant record turning on its roof; crates of records to throw like frisbees, a chair to sit and listen."],["MOTORIK","a lone petrol station under a cold-lit canopy on the long straight east road, a vending machine, and one car that drives the straight for ever."],["The Fuzz Field","five giant pedals to stomp (FUZZ, WAH, PHASE, ECHO, DOOM) in front of a wall of amps that glows hotter with each one."],["The Love-In","painted vans round a fire, rugs and cushions, strings of coloured bulbs, people dancing."],["The Sound Garden","a ring of nine giant chimes to strike, each its own colour."],["The Light Show","a white dome full of slowly drifting blobs of colour, beanbags, a projector whose slide you can change."],["The Mirror Spiral","mirrored walls winding in to a prism you can spin."],["The Mushroom Tea House","a round room inside a giant red mushroom, cushions round a low table."],["The High Point","a bench and a kaleidoscope on the hill above the long road."]]},"The Folk Woods":{"arriving":"A clear, misty morning, the sun low in the east. You arrive beside the Great Tree, a huge old oak with its roots spreading wide, a round wooden stage built round its trunk, log benches facing it, lanterns hung in its lower branches and a harmonica left on a stump. Past it lies a mountain lake, dug into the land, with mist drifting on the water.","vibe":"Gentle, warm and a little enchanted. The Old Giants, a handful of colossal red-trunked sequoias, rise far above the pines and oaks and make the land's skyline, a ring of lanterns round each foot. Golden shafts of morning sun slant down into the clearings, and glowing seeds drift on the air around you. A little steam train runs a loop of track round the lake (from the shared clock, so friends see the same train), puffing steam, past a wooden station with a \"PEACE TRAIN\" sign and a water tower. A long wooden footbridge on piles crosses the lake, lanterns along it. Canoes are pulled up on the shore among reeds and rocks; log cabins with lit windows send up chimney smoke; campfires crackle with logs round them; birds circle over the lake. Lantern-lit dirt trails branch out from the Great Tree to every artist. \"Peace Train\" strings the carriages with changing lights and speeds the train up; \"Rocky Mountain High\" turns the light golden and sends shooting stars through the morning sky; \"The Wreck of the Edmund Fitzgerald\" rolls a storm over the lake, dark, wet and windy; \"Bridge over Troubled Water\" lights the bridge's rails and turns the sun shafts soft and bright.","places":[["The Fire Tower","in the north-east: forty steps in four flights round a timber tower to a roofed deck with a bench over the whole wood."],["The Orchard","rows of apple trees, a basket of apples to carry off, a cider press to turn, barrels and stumps to sit on."],["The Beekeeper's Glade","white hives humming with gold motes and a honey stall glowing amber."],["The Storytelling Ring","stumps round a little fire, and a teller of tall tales who never quite finishes one."],["The Woodcarver's Shed","a carved bear, an owl and a fish, a whirligig to spin, and a rocking horse to sit on."]]},"The Yacht Rock Riviera":{"arriving":"An endless golden-pink sunset over a calm bay, the sun itself sitting big and low on the horizon in a soft glow, a path of glittering light running across the water toward it. The land had no sea of its own, so a bay is dug into its heart: you arrive on the shore looking out over it, a lamp-lit stone promenade with a white balustrade, date palms and teak benches running all the way round.","vibe":"Smooth and a little classy (and a little cheesy, on purpose). A marina with a main pier laid out like a piano keyboard (white planks, black keys raised between), finger jetties with sailing yachts and motor yachts rocking at their moorings; the white Riviera Yacht Club on the shore with a blue-and-white striped awning over a terrace of round tables, string lights and a flagpole; a red-and-white lighthouse on a point; a sandy beach with white grand pianos, striped deck chairs and parasols. White villas with terracotta roofs, pools and bougainvillea on the slopes, cypress and olive trees, vineyards in rows. Gulls wheel over the bay and now and then a sailboat glides across it. \"Rocket Man\" launches Elton's rocket; \"Africa\" brings rain over the bay and sets the drums glowing; \"Hotel California\" deepens the light to a dusky red and blazes the hotel's windows; \"Margaritaville\" blazes the tiki lights; \"Danger Zone\" sends a jet screaming low round the bay. The sailboats carry white sails. Every artist's area answers its own songs with a glow that wakes while the song plays.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the bay, the promenade and every artist left where they were. Four more pale stone roads with seaside lamps, palms and a teak bench half-way reach the quiet hills: west past the winery to the Lido, south to the tennis club, north to the studio, east to the gazebo. Along them, with the Riviera's people to talk to:","places":[["The Lido","a glowing turquoise pool in a pale deck, a diving board, striped loungers to lie on, a cabana bar with stools, a beach ball and pool rings to throw."],["The Riviera Lawn Tennis Club","a clay court, two players who have been at forty-love since lunch, an umpire's chair to sit in, a ball machine that lobs a ball when pressed, a basket of balls to throw."],["The Winery","a terrace of parasol tables under a vine-hung pergola with string lights, a white cellar house with a terracotta roof, barrels (one to tap), grapes to carry off, rows of vines."],["Smooth Sound Studios","a studio to walk into, a mixing desk with faders to push and meters that jump, a blinking recording light, an electric piano to sit at, a sofa, gold records, a saxophonist on take forty-one."],["The Sunset Gazebo","on the east rise: a bench facing the bay and the sun, a brass telescope."]]},"The Prog Marches":{"arriving":"A wide, strange marchland under a total eclipse: low in the sky a black sun hangs in a blazing corona, the sky is deep violet overhead and amber at the horizon, and the stars are out. You arrive before the Prism, a see-through crystal 20 m tall on a dark stone dais, faint light planes inside it: a white beam falls into it out of the sky and leaves it split into six ribbons of coloured light that run away over the valley and fade into the air, motes of colour drifting off them.","vibe":"Thoughtful and a little unsettling, and above all Pink Floyd's: they are the heart of the land (the Prism itself is theirs), and every Floyd song on the playlist is a place out in the marches, reached by its own trail, with a stone sign and a glow that wakes while its song plays. The hall of clocks (\"Time\"): clocks of every kind on posts round a grandfather clock with a swinging pendulum, all their hands spinning wildly when it plays. The wall (\"Comfortably Numb\"): a long white brick wall with a hole knocked through it and bricks scattered in front, a lone guitarist standing on top under a spotlight that blazes during the song. The sheep pen (\"Sheep\"): a dry-stone pen of grazing sheep whose eyes glow red. The office in the marsh (\"Have a Cigar\"): a big desk and a leather chair on a red rug, a gold record on an easel, a giant cigar smoking in a giant ashtray. The orrery (\"Astronomy Domine\"): planets on brass arms wheeling round a glowing sun. The green glade (\"Green Is the Colour\"): tall glowing green flowers round a little dark pool, a bench. The tor (\"Fearless\"): a rocky tor with stone steps spiralling up to a lantern on the summit. The fishbowl (\"Wish You Were Here\"): a giant glass bowl with two glowing fish swimming round in it. There's plenty of open marsh left for the prog still to come. A paved road loops round the Prism in a long figure of eight under tall chrome lamps with amber globes. Standing stones are set out in a ring in 7/8 (two, two, three, three bars of it), violet runes on them sparking on the beat. Giant brass and chrome gears turn half-sunk in the ground beside a fallen clock face that keeps the real time. Pipe organs stand in the fields, their pipes glowing with the music. A chrome Möbius ribbon stands on its edge; monoliths float and slowly turn over rings of stones. The rest is open marchland: heather and dry grass, twisted bare trees, still pools that hold the violet sky, cairns and boulders. \"Time\" sets the gears racing, the clock hands spinning and the runes flashing; \"Mr. Blue Sky\" lets the eclipse pass and turns the sky blue; \"Wish You Were Here\" sets the burning man blazing; \"Any Colour You Like\" cycles the spectrum and the organ pipes through every colour; \"YYZ\" sets the beacon flashing fast and pulses the light red; \"Inca Roads\" brings the saucer down low over its terraces. It's also the shore closest to the Everything Hub.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, lightly, so there is still plenty of open marsh for the prog to come (most of the south is left alone). Three paved roads under the chrome lamps run out from the figure of eight: north to the tip of the land, east along the arm toward the Hub, and south up to the heights. The low marsh is pale and the heights are dark. Lanes lead off to the places, and the people who never left will tell you about side two:","places":[["The Listening Room","a hi-fi lounge with two great speakers, shelves of records, a sofa, and the armchair in the sweet spot; drop the needle and the cones glow with it."],["The Floating Isles","at the north tip: stepping stones climb to a bench on a rock that hangs over the sea, while four more isles drift and turn beyond it."],["The Great Keyboard","keys the size of doors that you play by walking on them, each lighting its own pipe of colour."],["The Tape Loop","two reel-to-reels and a loop of tape running round the garden between them; start it and the reels turn."],["The Ferry Jetty","at the east end: a bench, a lamp, a moored boat and a telescope trained on the Hub."],["The Tea Garden","a lawn inside a hedge, tea laid for four, croquet hoops and balls to knock about."],["The Stair in Seven","on the southern heights: four flights of seven steps round a tower to a bench in the sky."]]},"The Iron Range":{"arriving":"Black and rust-brown mountains under a thunderstorm, lit from below by forge glow. You arrive in a ring of iron braziers before a pair of rusted gates hung open on stone posts under an iron \"THE IRON RANGE\" beam, and through them you see the Forge Peak, the tallest mountain in the world, raised right behind the arrival. High on its flank the forge mouth glows, a great cave framed in riveted iron girders with chains slung across it and an anvil the size of a house before it, a hammer resting on top, and from it a river of molten metal runs down the mountain between dark stone banks into a lava pool beside the arrival. Riveted iron bridges carry the trails over it.","vibe":"Loud, heavy, dramatic, and the hardest climb in the world. Black storm clouds race low overhead and lightning strikes now and then, flashing the whole range white; rain blows through; the forge breathes out embers and smoke; the molten river throbs with the music and spits sparks. Mine portals honeycomb the slopes, timber-framed tunnel mouths with a lantern over each and tracks running out to an ore cart. Dead pines, black crags, skull-shaped rocks with burning eyes, cracks in the ground glowing orange, old anvils, chains slung between posts, braziers along the gravel trails. \"Enter Sandman\" makes the forge erupt and brings the lightning thick and fast; \"Master of Puppets\" sets the puppet strings blazing red; \"War Pigs\" burns the sky red and makes the arcs roar; \"115\" floods the range with green light from the meteorite; \"Crazy Train\" sends the carts racing; \"Ace of Spades\" spins the card, throwing sparks. The storm comes and goes: during about half the songs a storm surge rolls in for the middle of the song (from the shared clock, so friends get the same storms), with heavier rain and lightning every few seconds, and even between surges the lightning comes often. Strikes often hit the tall burning beacons that mark every artist, throwing sparks; the beacons are how you find the artists across the dark range, which stays dark on purpose. Every artist's songs glow up while they play.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the forge, the molten river and every artist left where they were. The ground now reads by height: black ash down by the sea, rust on the slopes, paler rock up high. Four more gravel roads with braziers, each with an iron bench half-way, reach the empty parts: north to the miners' camp, east round the back of the Forge Peak and down the coast to the spit, south to the roadhouse, west along the high arm. Along them, with the people of the range to talk to:","places":[["Hammertown","the miners' camp: a smithy with an anvil to strike and a grindstone to spin, a \"TEST YOUR METAL\" striker whose top mark is eleven, a campfire with logs to sit on, tents, lumps of ore to carry off."],["The Signal Fire","on the north point: a stone cairn with an iron basket to light or smother, and a bench."],["The Ore Dock","on the east coast: a riveted crane whose hook swings when you push it, ore heaps, carts, ingots to carry (one of them gold)."],["The Dragon's Bones","on the south-east spit: a pale skeleton as long as a ship, curled round a heap of gold with its skull turned back toward you, eyes still burning; ribs to walk under, goblets to take, the tail to sit on, an old knight on watch four hundred years too late."],["The Rusty Nail","a roadhouse to walk into, bar stools, a jukebox to kick, an arm-wrestling table, three bikes out front to sit on."],["The Wall of Amps","on the west arm: a stage before eighteen amplifiers, a crowd that has been waiting for years, and a volume dial that goes to eleven."],["The Doom Bell","at the end of the west road: a great iron bell in a timber frame; toll it and the light rolls out over the range."]]},"The Southern Rock Bayou":{"arriving":"A juke joint on stilts under a giant live oak hung with Spanish moss: a tin roof, a deep porch with rocking chairs and a slide guitar, string lights out to the plaza, a fire pit ringed with logs, motorcycles and a pickup out front, a stage inside. A slow brown river winds past it from the hills down to the sea.","vibe":"Swampy and natural, humid and hazy, in a thick golden-green afternoon light (wet and green where the Delta Crossroads will be dry red dirt). Side channels branch off the river to still lagoons with lily pads; the river runs in a gentle valley; bald cypress stand in the shallows with their knees poking up; palmettos, reeds and cattails crowd the banks. Dirt roads with lanterns on crooked posts lead to every porch, crossing the water on plank bridges. Between them: tin fishing shacks on stilts, docks with jon boats, herons, campfires ringed with lawn chairs, hammocks, a rusted truck in the weeds, and a bonfire site on the highest hill. Fireflies drift everywhere, thicker in quiet songs, and now and then an alligator cruises the river. \"Free Bird\" sends a flock up off Skynyrd's roof, wheeling away over the swamp; \"Bad Moon Rising\" brings a huge moon up over the trees; \"(Ghost) Riders in the Sky\" sends pale riders galloping across the sky; \"Proud Mary\" has the paddle steamer cast off and roll down the river and back; \"Fire on the Mountain\" sets the hilltop bonfire roaring with sparks; \"Born on the Bayou\" thickens the haze and brings out the fireflies in thousands. Out in the swamp the biggest bands' songs are places, each reached by its own path (over plank bridges where it crosses water) with a hand-painted sign and a lantern, each glowing while its song plays. Allman Brothers Band: an old blue convertible parked on a stretch of open road, headlights on (\"Jessica\"), a bench on a rise inside a ring of blue lanterns (\"Blue Sky\"), and the sunburst over their porch shining for \"Soulshine\". Lynyrd Skynyrd: a tall dead cypress with a white bird taking off from the top (\"Free Bird\"), and a little white farmhouse with a porch swing, a mailbox, a vegetable patch and a lit window (\"Simple Man\"), its porch light and \"HOME\" over the steps glowing for \"Sweet Home Alabama\". The Marshall Tucker Band: a railroad crossing whose red lights flash (\"Can't You See\"), a jukebox glowing at the end of a dock (\"Heard It in a Love Song\"), an eagle on its nest at the top of a tall snag (\"Fly Eagle Fly\"), and their rainbow glowing for \"Searchin' for a Rainbow\". On the smaller porches: Little Feat's moon for \"Spanish Moon\", the Outlaws' green tide pool (\"Green Grass & High Tides\"), lanterns along 38 Special's rope swing (\"Hold On Loosely\"), a neon \"BAD\" sign (\"Bad To The Bone\"), glowing footprints walking away (\"Walk Away\"), a red clay pot of fire (\"Wondering Why\"). Inside, every porch house is lived in: a braided rug, a sagging sofa, a little table with an oil lamp, guitars hung on the wall, a framed gold record, an old radio.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with everything that was already here left where it was. Three more dirt roads with lanterns run out into the quiet parts, each keeping to one bank: south down the west bank to the tip of the land, south down the east bank to the river mouth, and north up the narrow strip in the north-west. Along them, with the neighbours to talk to:","places":[["The Fish Camp","at the south tip: a crawfish boil on a long table under string lights, a boil pot steaming, a fire ringed with lawn chairs, a dock with a bench at its end."],["The General Store","a deep porch with rocking chairs, an ice chest, cold drinks, and a tyre swing to push on the live oak beside it."],["The Smokehouse","a pit smoking under a tin roof, picnic tables, and a horseshoe pit with horseshoes to throw."],["The Airboat","pulled up on the east bank: sit on its bench, spin its fan."],["The Church in the Wildwood","white boards and a little steeple, a bell to ring by its rope, dinner on the grounds at two long tables."],["The Still in the woods","a copper still over a fire, jugs to carry off, a lawn chair, a sleeping hound to pet."]]},"The Hair Metal Strip":{"arriving":"Electric violet dusk over a red desert plateau, the sun just gone and the horizon still burning orange. You arrive under a neon arch, \"THE STRIP\" in pink and gold, beside a giant hot-pink hairspray can. The Strip runs away ahead: a dark boulevard with a pink centre line, palms ringed with neon, chrome lamps with pink tubes, motorbikes parked in rows at the kerb.","vibe":"Loud and silly in the best way, turned all the way up. A venue for every artist down both sides, and no two alike: each has its own wall colour, facade (red brick, chrome panels, racing stripes, painted flames, leopard spots, starfields, mirror tiles) and awning (leopard, zebra, candy stripes, black), and each glows up for its own songs. The desert either side is full: mesas, saguaros, hairspray-can hoodoos, billboards on chrome frames, chrome flying-Vs, a guitar-shaped swimming pool glowing turquoise with deck chairs, motel signs blinking \"NO VACANCY\", and cacti outlined in neon. open at the front over a stage with a glowing neon backdrop, a marquee of chasing bulbs, a leopard-print awning and the name in neon on the roof. At the far end the Club, built as a giant amp head (gold piping, a woven grille, chrome knobs, a carrying handle), with a big stage inside and pyro jets on the roof. Round the Strip: red mesas, saguaros, more giant hairspray cans standing like hoodoos, leopard-print billboards (\"TEASE IT UP\", \"BIGGER HAIR\"), chrome flying-V sculptures. Motorbikes cruise the Strip with their headlights on; the hairspray cans puff clouds of pink and gold glitter now and then; the Club's pyro fires in loud songs. \"Kickstart My Heart\" sets the Club's pyro roaring and flames bursting along the Strip; \"November Rain\" brings rain, blue light and rose petals drifting over the Guns N' Roses stage; \"The Final Countdown\" counts the red display down from ten, then fireworks burst over the Club; \"Livin' On A Prayer\" blazes every marquee and drops pink and gold confetti along the Strip.","places":[["The Motel","a pink court of rooms round a glowing pool, loungers, a tall neon sign; the television is there to be carried and thrown in the pool."],["Tease","the salon: a pink shop to walk into with a checkered floor, three dryer chairs whose hoods glow when you sit under them, a mirror ringed with bulbs, cans of hairspray to carry off, a hairspray can puffing on the roof."],["The Chapel of Love","a little white chapel with a pink roof and a beating neon heart, a bell to ring, pews, a couple being married for the ninth time, a bouquet to throw."],["The Video Shoot","out among the mesas: a band miming on a rock stage before a painted sunset, a wind machine to switch on (streamers fly), a camera crane, a director's chair to sit in."],["The Shred Shed","six pointy guitars on the wall to take down, carry and throw; the amp out front says SMASH HERE."],["Makeout Point","at the end of the Canyon Road: giant white letters, BIG HAIR, lit on the ridge; a pink convertible to sit in with a radio to turn, a telescope, the coast below."]]},"The Bluegrass Hollows":{"arriving":"A bright, clear summer afternoon in an Appalachian holler: steep green ridges rise on both sides of the creek bottom, blue haze on the far ridges, a pale moon hanging in the daytime sky. You arrive in the middle of the picking circle: seven rocking chairs round a lantern, and floating above them a banjo, a fiddle, a mandolin, a guitar and an upright bass, circling slowly, bobbing and swaying as if somebody were playing them. Behind it stands a big red barn with a gambrel roof and white trim, its great doors swung open on a porch stage with string lights, a mic and two rockers; inside, hay bales, a hayloft and a warm light.","vibe":"Quick-fingered and homespun, old-time and new at once, and a little bit magic. It must never feel like the Folk Woods next door: this is a working mountain valley, not a forest. Beside the picking circle a gravel main street runs along the creek through a little mountain town: a clapboard general store with a tall false front, its deep porch full of pickers in rocking chairs (banjo, fiddle, guitar, mandolin, a bass player standing at the end) under string lights, crates, a barrel and a drinks cooler beside them; a white clapboard church with a steeple, a bell and lit tall windows; the Old-Time Fiddlers' Convention stage with bunting, string lights, a fiddle on a stand and rows of hay bales to sit on; a brick post office with a flag; an old red gas pump with a lit globe and a tin shed; painted houses with porches; power poles with street lamps and sagging wires down the street. Up on the highest open ridge a coal mine's timber portal (\"MINE No. 9\") runs into the hillside, a cart track and a loaded coal cart coming out, a lantern and a coal heap, mist at its mouth. Downstream, a timber railroad trestle crosses the creek on splayed bents with X braces, three coal hoppers standing on it, the track running on onto both banks to a red bumper. Moonshine stills smoke quietly in the woods: copper pots over stone fires, cooling barrels, jugs. A creek winds through the hollow past the barn, ferns and stones on its banks, with a pool at each end. The trail across it goes through a red covered bridge with white portals and a lamp inside; farther along, plank footbridges. A grist mill on the bank turns a big wooden water wheel. Mason jar lamps light the dirt trails, split-rail fences zig-zag beside them, and the hollows are full of broadleaf trees in every summer green, pink rhododendrons, hay bales in the meadows, beehives, little corn patches, an old pickup, and porch cabins with rockers, lit windows and chimney smoke. A mule pulls a covered wagon along the longest trail and back (from the shared clock, so friends see the same wagon), and butterflies flutter everywhere. \"Foggy Mountain Breakdown\" rolls fog down into the hollows and sets the instruments whirling; \"Blue Moon Of Kentucky\" deepens the sky to blue and makes the moon glow big and blue; \"Super Moon\" swells the moon huge; \"Big Rock Candy Mountain\" sets the candy mountain sparkling and the lemonade spring fizzing; \"Wagon Wheel\" speeds the wagon up; \"I'm a Man of Constant Sorrow\" brings grey skies and rain; \"The Ballad of Jed Clampett\" makes the oil derrick gush; \"Wildfire\" sends sparks up from the fire lookout. Glows tied to songs brighten while their song plays (see the artists below), and mist lies low round the old-timers.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the town, the creek and every picker left where they were. Three more dirt trails with jar lamps reach the quiet parts without crossing the creek: a ridge road north up the mountain, a west road down to the low meadow, a short south road up the hill. Along them, with the valley's people to talk to:","places":[["The Square Dance","a lantern-hung pavilion, four couples in a square, a caller on a crate and a fiddler who only knows the fast ones; benches down both sides."],["The Luthier's Workshop","banjos, fiddles, a mandolin, a guitar and a dulcimer hung along the wall; pluck one and its strings shine."],["The Quilting Porch","a cream farmhouse with a quilt on the frame, rocking chairs, and quilts on the line in every colour, bright against the green."],["The High Lonesome","the top of the ridge: a bench under a lone pine, a few old stones, a jar lamp, the whole valley below."],["The Horse Pasture","in the west meadow: a split-rail paddock, four horses to pat, a water trough, a hitching rail."],["The Schoolhouse","one red room to walk into, desks to sit at, a lesson on the board, a bell to ring by the door."]]},"Arena Rock Ridge":{"arriving":"Deep night on a long pine ridge, the one true night among its neighbours: a dark blue sky full of stars, white searchlights sweeping it. You arrive at ticket booths and turnstiles under a lit \"ARENA ROCK RIDGE · TONIGHT\" arch, tour posters on boards. Roads lit by tall white stage lamps wind round the ridge.","vibe":"Big, earnest, epic and heroic. The stadium is the heart, and a live show is always on in it: a band in silhouette on stage (a singer at the mic, two guitarists, a drummer up on a riser behind his kit), each in a spotlight; a crowd of hundreds on the field swaying in waves, lighters held up, blazing for the anthems; lasers fanning out from the stage over the crowd and the sky (cold blue for the Reaper); flame jets firing along the stage front on the big beats; fireworks bursting over the stands near the end of the anthems (\"Don't Stop Believin'\", \"Carry on Wayward Son\", \"Renegade\", \"Eye of the Tiger\", \"More Than a Feeling\", \"Wheel in the Sky\", \"We Built This City\"), from the shared clock so friends see the same ones; a merch tent with glowing shirts outside the gate. On the highest part of the ridge a crumbling stadium stands like a castle, its hilltop cut flat: battlement stands with a gateway facing the road in, banks of seats packed with thousands of lighter flames, four floodlight towers, and a big stage with a truss light rig, two screens and amp stacks, searchlights sweeping up from it. Round the ridge: pines and rock outcrops, a lot of tour buses, tailgate parties with string lights and coolers, piles of road cases, speaker stacks facing out over the valley. The lighters glow with the song; the rig chases; Journey's wheel turns in the sky. \"(Don't Fear) The Reaper\" turns the light cold and blue, slows the searchlights and lights the reaper's eyes; \"Don't Stop Believin'\" sends the midnight train along its track and makes the stadium's lighters blaze in waves; \"Come Sail Away\" lifts the Styx ship into the sky; \"Eye of the Tiger\" lights the tiger's eye and swings the searchlights onto the boxing ring; \"More Than a Feeling\" sends the guitar spaceship on a wide loop over the ridge.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the stadium, the roads and every band left where they were. Five more lamp-lit asphalt roads close the eastern dead ends into a ring and reach the empty parts. Along them, with the people of the ridge to talk to:","places":[["The Ridge Drive-In","on the east lobe: a great white screen playing a concert film (the band in silhouette, swaying), two rows of cars with their lights on to sit in, speaker posts, a striped snack shack with tubs of popcorn to carry off."],["The Arcade","a dark hall edged in pink and cyan neon to walk into; six cabinets to play (each tells you how you did), an air hockey table with a puck to hit, regulars who can't talk right now."],["The Ballad Pool","a still round pool of floating lights with benches round it; raise the lighter on the plinth and the lights lift off the water for a while."],["Backstage","a catering tent with long tables to sit at, stacked road cases, a rack of guitars with a tech tuning them, a follow spot to swing."],["Lighter Hill","on the south rise: a lighter as tall as a house that flicks on and lights the hillside, benches looking back over the ridge."]]},"Metalcore Caldera":{"arriving":"A basin of black obsidian and pale ash ringed by five volcanoes, their craters and lava running not orange but neon: cyan, violet and magenta. You arrive on a basalt circle ringed with cyan light and neon posts, before an obsidian slab with \"METALCORE CALDERA\" in cyan, a toppled amp stack and two steam vents. Just beyond is the pit arena: a round black floor with neon rings set into it, a round stage of amp stacks under a ring truss of lights in the middle, four rings of obsidian stands rising round it (open toward the arrival), four light towers, and a crowd of dark figures running a circle pit round the stage, faster as the music builds.","vibe":"Cathartic release. Smoky and overcast, lit violet from below; ash drifts down all the time. The land builds and breaks like the music: during about half the songs, at the breakdown (from the shared clock, so friends see the same), the cracks in the ground throb faster and faster and the lava brightens, then the volcanoes erupt, throwing neon sparks and violet smoke from every crater under a magenta sky while the lava streams blaze; afterwards everything glows quietly and the ash falls thicker. \"My Curse\", \"To Hell And Back\" and \"God Complex\" always erupt. \"BLACKOUT\" blacks the neon out in stutters; \"Happiness by the Kilowatt\" makes every neon post blaze; \"Spiritual Eclipse\" raises a black disc with a violet corona over the basin; \"BIRDS\" sends a flock wheeling over Turnstile's wire; \"Meet Me At The Gates\" and \"Afterlife\" send pillars of light up from the gates and the door. Everywhere: obsidian spires with neon tips, clusters of neon crystals, steam vents, glowing cracks, toppled amps, chain-link pens, cinder boulders and drifts of pale ash, with basalt paths edged in cyan studs and neon posts. Every one of the songs has its own light that brightens while it plays.","places":[["The Rage Room","a chain-link pen under magenta strip lights with a sprayed target wall. Old televisions, plates, bottles, an office keyboard and a length of pipe, all to pick up and throw."],["The Wall of Things Unsaid","a long curved wall of obsidian slabs, each with a line in neon handwriting that is dim until you walk up to it (\"i should have called\", \"it got better\", \"sorry about the van\"). At a booth in front you can write a line of your own; the whole wall lights for a moment."],["Black Ink","a tattoo and merch bunker to walk into. A chair to sit in, flash sheets to choose from, a rail of band shirts (black) to carry off."],["Scream Point","on the east rim: a round obsidian platform with one microphone facing the whole caldera. Scream, and a ring of magenta light rolls out over the basin."]]},"The Alt Rock Moors":{"arriving":"Grey-green moorland under a moody sky with a low, warm sun, wide golden breaks of light drifting slowly across it. You arrive at a crossroads of moor roads with a red phone box, a lonely bus shelter, a white fingerpost and sodium street lamps. On the tallest hill, raised higher, stands a giant red-and-white lattice radio transmitter, 90 m tall and guyed with cables, its orange warning lights blinking together up its height. Close by, across the moor, rises a block of concrete with a tower: Radiohead's district.","vibe":"Moody, quiet and a little uncanny, made to wander. Radiohead's district is walked through like an exhibition, a ring of nine concrete spaces, each built round a song: the station at the front (song titles changing on the walls, a wall of televisions full of static); a deep-water room with light rippling on its ceiling, bubbles rising and a lone microphone in a spotlight; an underground platform where a tram comes out of one tunnel, waits and leaves into the other; a dark room where it rains indoors under a red ring of neon, a single chair in the middle; a white room full of mist with a pale figure who flickers in and out, its back door opening onto the moor; an aquarium of glowing fish with floor lights playing an arpeggio; an open court with a wall of coloured jigsaw pieces, a few loose ones drifting; a wind room of hanging white ribbons with a dinosaur skeleton in a glass case and a coat and hat on a stand; and in the middle, under a roof of strip lights, a grid of white cubes all in line but one. Out on the moor: a flooded reservoir with a drowned village on its floor and a stepped pyramid rising out of it, glowing fish swimming in the air above; a white motorway that stops in mid-air; a city of coloured blocks with words on their sides; a cluster of sharp icy peaks; a grove of gnarled trees covered in glowing eyes; an old car driving the longest road and back. Pylons striding across with sagging wires, wind-bent hawthorns and gorse in drifts.","roads":"Laid out on purpose over the whole land. A loop of moor road runs right round the artists, with three roads out to it from the crossroads; an east road follows the coastal strip to the far tip, a north-west road climbs the headland, and a south road runs down the tongue. The roads have a broken white line and cat's eyes glinting on the verges, and benches with lamps at intervals on the side that looks downhill. Dry-stone-walled fields line them, bright grass and buttercups inside, sheep in half of them, against the dark heather of the tops; a pale tor crowns each real hilltop. Gravel lanes lead off to the places and to every strange thing on the moor. Quiet locals live here: they look round as you pass and say something dry if you say hi.","places":[["Little Static, the village",""],["The sheep farm","on the south tongue: farmhouse, stone barn, a walled fold full of sheep with a gate to open, the shepherd and the dog."],["Moor Halt","on the east strip: a little platform with a shelter, a signal to pull, a suitcase someone left, and a two-car train that comes, waits and goes (on the clock, so everyone sees it in the same place)."],["The lookout","on the north-west headland: trig point, toposcope, a telescope to swing round, a cairn with a lantern, walkers with a flask."],["TEA. JUST TEA","a tea van by the sea with tables looking out, a kite flying over it."],["The stone circle","nine stones that light when you touch them; light them all and they breathe with light together."],["The waiting room","with no building: rows of chairs on a square of lino in the heather, NOW SERVING 3, a ticket machine, a flickering strip light."],["The desk","an office cubicle alone on the moor, its screen still on, a swivel chair to sit in, paper planes to throw."],["The marsh of lights","a zigzag boardwalk over dark pools where pale lights drift (\"keep to the boards, the lights lie\")."],["The phone box that rings","alone on the far moor: answer it."],["The last bench","at the far east tip, facing the sea."]]},"The Punk Badlands":{"arriving":"A cracked cul-de-sac turning circle at a harsh, bright noon: a basketball hoop on a pole, a row of battered mailboxes, shopping carts, a green town sign (\"WELCOME TO THE PUNK BADLANDS, POP. EVERYONE\") with a tag sprayed across it. Just beyond, a giant drained swimming pool turned skatepark.","vibe":"Teenage summer: skateboards, suburbs, sunburn, being sixteen. Sun-bleached badlands under a white-hot sky, flat-topped mesas striped with bands of red, cream and ochre rock, scrub, and the ruins of a suburb the skaters took over. The pool has white coping, sky-blue tiles, a ladder, the stump of a diving board, graffiti all over its floor, a fence and a lifeguard chair, and kids skating its walls. Smaller skate bowls carved into the dirt, wooden ramps and kickers, roofless house shells with garage doors hanging off, graffiti walls, couches left out, swing sets, trampolines, cars on blocks, an ice-cream truck, tumbleweeds rolling through. Cracked sidewalks with streetlights and fire hydrants. Checkerboard floors for the ska bands.","happenings":"Kids skate the pool and the bowls (the same skaters for everyone); tumbleweeds roll through. \"Wake Me up When September Ends\" brings autumn leaves and gold light; \"Boulevard of Broken Dreams\" dims the land to a lonely blue and the boulevard's lamps flicker; \"Welcome to the Black Parade\" darkens the sky and black confetti falls; \"99 Red Balloons\" sends red balloons up across the land; \"High Hopes\" lets balloons go from the top of the ladder.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the cul-de-sac, the pool and every driveway left where they were. Four more cracked sidewalks under streetlights run out to the empty east, north, south-west and west, with a couch, a mailbox or a shopping cart left under the lights (the couches can be sat on). The kids who never left live along them; it has been noon on the last day of school for years.","places":[["Badlands High, the gym on prom night","a brick gym to walk into: a basketball court under streamers and a balloon arch, coloured lights, a mirror ball to spin, a stage with a DJ and a banner, bleachers to sit on, a punch table (cups to carry), basketballs, a chaperone by the door. A school bus that never leaves outside."],["Sal's Pizza","a parlour with a checkered floor, an oven glowing behind the counter, two booths, a jukebox to kick, and a stack of pizza boxes to carry off. A picnic table outside."],["The TP'd House and the Legal Wall","the one neat house in the Badlands, on the one green lawn, buried in toilet paper; its owner sits in a lawn chair with a hose, the culprit hides behind the tree, and a crate of rolls waits to be thrown over the roof. Beside it a long grey wall: spray it and a tag appears each time, until someone from the city paints it grey again."],["The Water Tower","a steel tank on four legs with the town's name on it and what the kids added. One long flight of stairs climbs to the catwalk: sit with your legs over the edge and look out, press play on the boombox."]]},"The Math Rock Escarpment":{"arriving":"A round wooden deck at the top of the land under string lights strung from four tall posts, wind chimes either side, a carved wooden sign. Just ahead the land breaks at the escarpment: a long cliff running right across it, with the sea and the falls beyond.","vibe":"Crisp autumn with long shadows, quiet between bursts of noise. Two sides to fit two kinds of music. Above the escarpment, the math rock side: warm and busy, red, orange and gold trees, leaf piles, string lights between posts, wind chimes everywhere, little painted sheds with lit windows, benches with lanterns, bikes leaning on posts. Below it, out toward the sea, the post-rock side: wide and quiet, tall pale grass, lone bare trees, standing stones, benches facing the sea, driftwood, mist. Streams run off the high ground and pour over the escarpment in waterfalls with mist at their feet; by the biggest fall a lone wooden house stands at the top of the cliff, one upstairs window lit. Paths of packed earth edged with fallen leaves, timber treads where they climb.","happenings":"Leaves fall, chimes sway, string lights twinkle with the music. In the post-rock songs the quiet side swells with the song, the lanterns, falls and sky brightening slowly toward the climax and settling at the end. \"Ashes in the Snow\" brings snow over the whole land; \"two moons\" hangs two moons in the sky; \"Supermoon\" raises a giant moon; \"春雨 Spring Rain\" brings a soft rain; \"Everlasting Light\" sends a beam up from MONO's lamp.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the deck, the scarp, the streams and every terrace and clearing left where they were. Three more packed-earth paths with lanterns and drifts of leaves reach the empty east, south and west. On the warm math-rock side above the scarp, with people to talk to:","places":[["The Yatai","a lantern-hung food stall on a deck: noren curtains, a steaming pot, stools to sit at, bowls of ramen to carry."],["The Counting Floor","a deck of tiles seven across and eleven long under string lights. A tile lights when you stand on it, and a pulse walks the floor on its own, counting; a metronome post changes the count (seven, eleven, five, plain four)."],["The Persimmon Orchard","bare-branched trees hung with bright orange fruit, the leaves all on the ground; a drying shed strung with persimmons glowing like lanterns; a ladder, baskets of fruit to carry."],["The Crescendo Field","one weathered piano in the long grass facing the sea, and forty pale lamps on thin poles, dark. Press one key and sit down: over most of a minute the lamps come on one by one, outward from the piano, to a blaze; it holds; then they all go out."]]},"The Grunge Rainwood":{"arriving":"A muddy crossroads of logging roads deep in a dripping pine forest on a high plateau, puddles in the ruts, yellow road lamps on creosoted poles hazy in the drizzle. A carved wooden sign under a little mossy roof, a coffee shack with one window lit and \"COFFEE\" over the door, a red logging truck loaded with logs, flannel shirts on a clothesline. Behind the arrival a mossy bluff rises, and a roaring white waterfall pours off it between black rock buttresses into a dark pool. Beside the pool stands the old sawmill, now the land's venue: a long timber hall with a tin roof, its big door slid open on a lit stage (amps, a drum riser, a mic, string lights, benches for the crowd), a giant saw blade ringed in light over the door, a rusted smokestack trailing smoke, a water wheel turning in its race and stacked logs by the wall.","vibe":"Heavy and moody. Cold blue-grey light under a low grey sky that drags over the treetops; it always drizzles, and during about half the songs a downpour rolls in for the middle of the song (from the shared clock, so friends get the same weather), with now and then a lightning strike. Mist drifts low between the pines and banks over the river; spray rises from the foot of the falls; crows lift out of the trees and circle over the falls during some songs. Warm yellow lamps against the blue-grey: lit trailer windows, phone booths, fire lookouts on their towers. The river runs from the pool through the forest down to the sea in rapids, crossed by plank bridges with log rails. Tall dark pines in thick stands with clearings between, fallen mossy logs, stumps, boulders, rusted trailers on blocks with lawn chairs and clotheslines, log piles, old cars sinking into the moss, benches under a lamp, ferns everywhere. The mill's stage lights and the saw blade's ring pulse with the music. \"Black Hole Sun\" stops the rain, turns the sky dark amber and raises a black sun with a burning rim over the falls; \"Lightning Crashes\" brings lightning again and again, often onto Live's lightning rod; \"No Rain\" stops the rain and lets gold light break through; \"Say Hello 2 Heaven\" sends a pillar of light up from the little temple; \"Disarm\" swings the bells; \"Even Flow\" brightens the river and the falls. Every one of the 40 songs has its own light that brightens while it plays.","places":[["The House Show","in the north-east: a rented house with the band in the living room (two guitars, a drummer, six people nodding), a porch with a sagging couch and a string of bulbs, a coffee can for the door money, a van that says \"wash me\"."],["The Logging Camp","in the east: a clear-cut of pale fresh stumps, the one bright open place in the forest. A bunkhouse, a cook fire under a tarp, a two-man saw stuck in a great log (pull it), and a target with four axes to throw."],["Last Chance Gas","in the south-west: a filling station gone to moss, its canopy sagging, one strip light flickering; a shop to walk into where the pinball machine still works (sometimes multiball), flat soda on ice, a bench."],["The Big Fella's clearing","in the north-west: giant footprints up the lane to a fire, and beside it on a fallen trunk somebody enormous, shaggy and shy with glowing headphones on. Say hi; sit on the log with him; his tapes are marked \"quiet ones\", \"loud ones\" and \"do not tape over\"."]]},"The Post-Punk Docklands":{"arriving":"Night-time melancholy. You arrive on a wet concrete square up on the plateau between brutalist tower blocks, under tall sodium lamps that pool orange light on the puddles. The tallest block, twelve floors of ribbed concrete with a lift tower and a blinking red light on top, is dark but for a single lit window high up. A cast concrete wall carries the land's name; a concrete bus shelter is pasted with flyers; a steel-and-glass phone box has its light on. West, the ground falls away to the harbour at sea level.","vibe":"A deep night of black water, wet concrete and sodium orange, with cold blue in between; no rain falls, there are only its reflections. Down at the harbour a concrete quay wall with bollards runs along the whole coast; three container cranes straddle it with red lights blinking on top and their booms out over the water; a cargo ship lies moored, its bridge and portholes lit, containers stacked on deck; container stacks, pallets and burning oil drums fill the quay; long corrugated warehouses have their roller doors half up and flyers on the walls; a breakwater of concrete blocks runs out to sea to a red-and-white lighthouse whose beam turns over the harbour, and mist lies on the water. Up on the plateau the centre is flat and laid out on a street grid running down to the harbour: wide asphalt streets with sodium lamps at the corners, kerbed blocks of tall towers, pairs of concrete slabs and brick terrace rows square to the streets; the artists' corners ring the grid; beyond the grid the ground slopes away to the docks and the edges. Around it: concrete and brick blocks with a few lit windows, terraced brick rows with chimneys, chain-link fences topped with barbed wire, skips, parked cars, street grates breathing steam, walls of torn flyers, concrete benches under a lamp. The lamps hum and now and then one flickers. \"There Is a Light That Never Goes Out\" puts out every street lamp in the docks, leaving the night black but for the one lit window, the lighthouse and an old car's headlights, which blaze. \"How Soon Is Now?\" makes the sodium lamps throb. \"99 Red Balloons\" sends ninety-nine red balloons rising slowly into the night. \"Everybody Wants To Rule The World\" sweeps searchlights from the cranes across the sky. \"The Sprawl\" lights every window in every tower. \"Golden Brown\" turns the night gold. \"Pink Steam\" pours pink steam from the grates. \"Thunder\" brings lightning down on Maruja's rod. Every one of the 39 songs has its own light that brightens while it plays.","places":[["The Flyover","over the north street: a concrete road in the air that ends in broken rebar and a red warning light. A flight of stairs climbs to it; at the broken end, a bench looking out over the harbour. Underneath, a strip-lit underpass with a mural on its piers, a busker with an open guitar case, and an echo: clap, and the lights jump."],["The Pressing Plant","in the north: a brick factory hall with a saw-tooth roof and a chimney. Two presses to pull (the platen comes down, a record glows hot), a belt carrying records toward the door, a crate of records still warm to carry off and throw, sleeves printed black on black."],["The Social","in the south: a working men's club to walk into. A bar with stools, a dartboard with three darts to throw, a bingo machine to call numbers on, a little stage with a tinsel curtain and a microphone, a couple who have had the same table for forty years."],["The Chippy","above the south quay: a small tiled shop open to the street under a neon fish, cones of chips to carry, a queue of two, and a low sea wall to sit on facing the black water."]]},"The Djent Spires":{"arriving":"A hexagonal plaza of white stone inlaid with gold, concentric hexagons narrowing to a small crystal, six crystal lanterns round it and a slate slab with the land's name framed like a fretboard. Straight ahead, on the highest summit, a great cluster of ice crystal, and floating far above it the fractal citadel: a diamond of crystal whose every face carries a smaller copy of itself, and every copy smaller copies again, turning slowly round a gold heart, joined to the spire's tip by a shaft of light.","vibe":"Precise and dazzling: the brightest of the metal lands, in crisp, cold, clear daylight. Pale cliffs stepped into clean terraces, crystal spires and clusters growing out of them, fallen crystal columns, gold survey markers. Paths are fretboards: dark slate with gold frets and pearl inlays. Cubes hang in precise grids, turning in step; Tesla coils crack arcs of light, more in loud songs; a footbridge strung with glowing strings spans a hollow. Reads by dark blue slate against pale ice, white stone and warm gold.","happenings":"\"The Sky Is Red\" turns the whole sky red; \"Electric Sunrise\" brings a gold dawn; \"The Summoning\" drains the light to dark crimson and the sigil blazes; \"Light\" flashes every crystal white on the beat; \"Odessa\" sets off a storm of Tesla arcs. The citadel turns by the shared clock, so friends see it at the same angle.","roads":"The land used to be pale on pale. Now the flat treads of the terraces are deep blue slate and the risers between them pale ice, so the land is striped dark and light all the way up, and the white stone, the crystal and the gold stand out against dark ground. Everything else is where it was. One more fretboard path, the East Scale, runs down the empty east side from north to south with crystal lanterns and white benches. Along it, with the people of the Spires to talk to:","places":[["The Polyrhythm Clock","a white and gold dais under four tilted gold rings turning three against four against five against seven, a bead of light flashing on each beat. A lever changes the tempo (andante, allegro, presto)."],["The Tuning Forks","eight tall crystal forks in a row, low to high. Strike one and it rings with light for a while."],["The Hex Garden","a hexagon of dark raked gravel in a white kerb round three crystals; a rake that leaves five lines of gold; stepping stones to a tea pavilion with cushions and bowls of tea. Please do nothing."],["The Observatory","a twelve-sided white drum under a dark dome with a slit, to walk into. A gold telescope to look through, an orrery to turn, star charts, a stool."]]},"The J-Rock Isles":{"arriving":"A square in the middle of a neon city at dusk: a crossing painted across it, red paper lanterns strung overhead, vending machines glowing, a cherry tree, and a big neon sign with the land's name. Streets run off in a grid between tall buildings; high above, a red torii stands on the tallest roof, and up on the hill a school and a shrine.","vibe":"Anime energy, like an opening sequence, and two halves to it. Below, the neon city: tall concrete buildings with rows of lit windows, vertical neon signs on their corners, water tanks and lit billboards on the roofs, a lit shopfront at every foot, vending machines on the corners, power poles with tangled wires and lanterns strung between them, cherry trees. Above, an anime hilltop town: little tiled houses, bamboo, stone lanterns, a long path up the hill lined with red torii to a small shrine with a sweeping roof and a view over the city, and a school partway up with a clock, a rooftop fence and a running track in its yard. A train runs along the coast on low piers, its crossing lights blinking as it comes. A dramatic dusk with big clouds lit orange and pink, cherry petals drifting down.","happenings":"The train runs out along the coast and back (the same train for everyone). \"Bad Apple!!\" drains the whole land to black and white; \"COLORS\" cycles the sky through every colour; \"FIRE BIRD\" sets the sky burning; \"Skye\" clears it to bright blue and the kite climbs; \"OXYGEN\" sends bubbles up round the stage.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the city, the shrine, the school and the train left where they were. Two more roads leave the city under power poles strung with paper lanterns, for the empty hills north and south. This is the episode where nothing happens and everyone goes to the festival; the people here are students in summer clothes who know they are in an opening sequence.","places":[["Isle Mart","a convenience store open all night, cold white light pouring out of a glass front. Aisles of bright packets, a wall of drinks fridges, a clerk; rice balls, canned coffee, melon bread and ramune to carry off. A bench and a vending machine outside."],["The Batting Centre","a long net cage under floodlights with a pitching machine at the far end. Press the button and it pitches one of the baseballs (fetch them back or it runs out); bats to carry; a HOME RUN target nobody has ever hit."],["The Summer Festival","a ring of six striped stalls round a red-and-white drum tower with lantern strings running out from its top. Climb up and beat the taiko (the whole place flashes), light the fireworks at the back, take a mask, shaved ice, takoyaki or rings to toss; people in summer robes walk the ring."],["Cat Hill","a little shrine through a torii to a giant white lucky cat whose paw waves. Ring the bell for a fortune. A dozen cats lie about, each to stroke; fish on a mat (for the cats)."]]},"The Oddball Backroads":{"arriving":"A crossroads on a hazy summer afternoon: a giant fibreglass head grinning on a plinth (its eyes follow you), a water tower leaning badly beside it with ODDBALL on the tank, a stop sign that says MAYBE, an old gas pump, a row of pink lawn flamingos, and a hand-painted sign: \"THE ODDBALL BACKROADS, you are probably here\".","vibe":"Weird and funny, a wink in everything. Two-lane back roads with a yellow line wind over low hills, telephone poles leaning along them with sagging wires, mailboxes, and road signs that are slightly wrong (\"SPEED LIMIT: VIBES\", \"BRIDGE MAY BE A DREAM\", \"DEER?\"). Kudzu swallows trees, poles, pickups and an old red barn. Flamingo flocks, junk sculptures, a thrift-store couch alone in a field with its lamp on, rusted pickups, corn patches, hay bales, a lone door standing in a field. Lots of room to get pleasantly lost.","roads":"Laid out on purpose over the whole land. A loop road circles the crossroads, a west road runs out over the gold plateau and back, a coast road runs down the shore, and a north road climbs to the lighthouse. Fields line the roads behind white fences (gold wheat, corn and sunflowers you can walk into, kudzu, a flamingo pasture), with hedgerows behind them. The road runs through kudzu tunnels, one lone tree stands on each hilltop, and dark kudzu groves fill the hollows. Gravel lanes lead off to the places, each with somewhere to sit, something to play with and the people who live there:","happenings":"The head watches you; dandelion fluff drifts in the haze. \"Ocean Man\" turns the sky ocean blue, \"Where Is My Mind?\" a dreamy violet, \"It's The End Of The World As We Know It\" red with meteors streaking down, \"Blister In The Sun\" white-hot, \"Yoshimi\" pink."},"The Nu Metal Quarry":{"arriving":"A gravel yard at the quarry gate: a site sign (\"THE NU METAL QUARRY, HARD HATS MUST BE WORN\"), a portable cabin with a lit window, oil drums burning, a floodlight tower; just ahead, the rim of a huge open pit and the haul road going down into it.","vibe":"2000s radio nostalgia, big earnest choruses on the car radio, under a flat grey overcast with dust in the air. The pit steps down in terraces, flat benches between steep cut rock faces, a haul road ramping down one side edged with a berm and marker posts, a giant haul truck crawling up and down it. On the benches: rusted haul trucks, excavators, rock piles, burning drums; a conveyor climbs out of the pit to a rust-red crusher on the rim; floodlight towers and chain-link round the edge. Round the rim, wrecked cars, spoil heaps, tyre stacks, cabins, dead trees, more drums.","happenings":"The haul truck runs the road (the same for everyone); rocks ride the conveyor; dust drifts in the pit; crows circle the rig. \"Kryptonite\" turns the light green; \"The Sound of Silence\" sinks the fires and darkens the sky; \"Photograph\" sets off camera flashes; \"Higher\" sends a beam up from Creed's tower; \"Stricken\" brings lightning down on the rod.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the gate, the pit and the rim left where they were. Two more gravel haul roads with yellow marker posts and sodium lamps run out over the empty south. The crew live along them, in hi-vis and hard hats; the radio only gets one station and it only plays one year.","places":[["The Blast Bench","a sandbagged tin shelter on a hilltop with a bench inside, wires running off to a heap of shot rock. Push the plunger: the siren winds up, then the heap goes up in a flash and dust and the loose rocks come down roughly where they went up."],["The Lot","the staff car park, five cars from 2003 nosed up to the view. Sit in a driver's seat, turn the key in the one with the stereo (the headlights come on), sit on the pickup's tailgate; cans in a cooler, CDs on a bonnet to throw."],["The Blue Hole","an old pit flooded bright turquoise under a cut rock face, the one piece of colour in the grey. A rope swing off a crane jib, a jumping rock with steps up the back, a drowned digger's arm reaching out of the water, an inner tube, towels on the bank."],["The Crib","the crew's canteen cabin up on blocks: two tables with benches, a tea urn, a radio to turn the dial on, a calendar stopped at August 2003, hard hats on hooks and lunch boxes to carry off."]]},"The Power Metal Peaks":{"arriving":"A round flagstone courtyard ringed with braziers and war banners, a standing stone carved with the land's name and a great sword driven into its top. Ahead, high on the tallest peak, a castle with a dragon coiled round its keep, wings spread, head out over the battlements.","vibe":"Over-the-top fun, epic and fierce all at once. Dark fantasy mountains sharpened into jagged snow-capped peaks under a blazing sunset (dark silhouettes against a fiery sky, where the Djent Spires next door is pale ice in daylight). Torch-lit flagstone paths; dark pines, banners, swords in stones, treasure chests, braziers, ruined watchtowers; and here and there a pop of kawaii neon (pink hearts, cyan stars), stone lanterns and cherry trees in blossom. On the coast, a stormy pirate cove: a lantern-lit pier, a pirate ship at anchor rolling in the swell, barrels, a chest, and a giant rubber duck bobbing in the water.","happenings":"The dragon breathes fire in about half the songs (the same moment for everyone), and all through \"Through the Fire and Flames\", when the sky burns red. Fireworks burst over HANABIE.'s rack in their songs and sometimes for no reason. Some songs bring a storm in off the sea, rain and lightning; \"Haten ni raimei\" always does. \"My Heart Will Go On\" turns the sky cold, \"Gimme Chocolate!!\" pink, and \"Drink\" lights every lantern up.","places":[["The Mead Hall","a great timber hall under a steep roof with gold dragon heads on the gable, to walk into. A long hearth, two long tables with benches to sit at, horns of mead to carry, a high seat, feasters, a bard on verse nine of a hundred and forty, and a great horn to sound (every horn in the hall goes up)."],["The Tournament Field","a sand strip with a tilt rail and banners, a quintain that whirls when struck, an archery butt, a rack of wooden swords to carry, a stand to sit in, a herald."],["The Wizard's Tower","a round stone tower under a tall crooked purple roof with a glowing orb. Inside: a crystal ball that answers (mostly about guitar solos), shelves of books and three that float, a bubbling cauldron, the wizard, a familiar to stroke."],["The Neko Metal Café","a pink café with a giant cat's head in a studded collar on the roof. Parfaits to carry, heart-backed chairs, warriors in full armour and kitten bibs, a capsule machine to turn, a tiny stage with a pink flying V."]]},"The Indie Downs":{"arriving":"Rolling chalk downs in faded, sun-bleached colour, like an old photo of a summer day. Golden late-summer grass in deep drifts, full of poppies, cornflowers and daisies; hedgerows, copses on the hilltops, sheep grazing, round hay bales in the fields, and a giant guitar cut into the white chalk of a hillside facing the hub's causeway. The light is low, warm and hazy, and seeds and pollen float through the air all round you, drifting faster when the wind is up: the Downs' own weather, the way snow is Lanternwood's.","vibe":"A late-summer afternoon you only half remember. Muted and changeable rather than sad. The weather follows the music more than on any other land: quiet songs cloud the sky over and the light goes soft and grey-blue; energetic songs bring the sun out warm, and the wind picks up, so the long grass and bunting race, the kites climb and the windmill's sails spin faster. Every new song starts with a gust. Some songs bring a surprise, the same for everyone there: a passing summer shower (likelier for quiet, sad songs) with a rainbow after it, a murmuration of starlings swirling over the hills, or a hot-air balloon drifting across the sky for the length of the song. Low clouds drift over, their shadows sweeping across the hills. Tall landmarks to find your way by: a white windmill, a flint church with a spire and a churchyard, a brick folly tower on a hilltop, and rows of tall poplars. At the heart is a village green: cottages in faded paint with their porch lamps already on, a maypole with bunting, a red phone box, a postbox, bicycles and lamp posts covered in gig posters, all glowing warm in the long afternoon. Out on the open downs: field hedges with five-bar gates, a shepherd's hut with its lamp lit, a half-remembered festival campsite with a fire going and bunting between the tents, big oaks with a picnic table and a lantern in the branches, beehives along a field edge, a scarecrow in the stubble, and a kite lost in a lone tree. Every artist area has its own warm light that swells while that artist plays.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, lightly: the two paths, the green and every artist are exactly where they were. Four plain chalk lanes with warm lanterns (in no one's colour) and a bench half-way reach the empty west, north-west, south-east and north-east. Along them, with a few people of the Downs to talk to:","places":[["The Beach Huts","above the west shore: five huts in faded paint, striped deckchairs facing the sea, a windbreak, an ice cream van with a giant cone on its roof, flat pebbles to pocket."],["The Allotments","six beds (cabbages, beans, leeks, sunflowers, pumpkins, cut flowers), two sheds, a greenhouse, a water butt and a wheelbarrow; a watering can and pumpkins to carry."],["The Car Boot Sale","five cars with their boots open and trestle tables of half-remembered things, every one of which can be picked up (a chipped teapot, a little lamp, a globe, a record with no label, a mixtape); sellers in deckchairs, bunting, teas at 20p."],["The Caravan Site","three old caravans with striped awnings round a patch of grass, deckchairs, a washing line, swingball to hit, a radio whose dial finds the shipping forecast."],["The Toposcope","on the north-east rise: a brass dial that tells you what lies each way, a trig pillar, a bench, a flask someone left."]]},"Lanternwood":{"arriving":"Birch woods dusted with snow, open clearings, and a frozen creek running down into a frozen lake you can walk out on. An old chapel full of candles, big enough for a group, with pews facing the altar.","vibe":"Deep blue dusk under a sky full of stars, light only from lanterns, windows, candles and fires. Snowmen stand beside the paths (somewhere new each day). A fire circle with log seats to gather round, and a lone bench on high ground looking out to sea. Hushed.","roads":"Out in the snowy woods, each down its own lantern-lit side path (two out on the frozen lake), small hushed places for the songs, each glowing and breathing while its song plays. Phoebe Bridgers: a rowing boat frozen into the ice, tilted (\"Motion Sickness\"); an old road ending in a snowbank, an END sign and a doorway of light beyond (\"I Know The End\"); a sheet ghost holding a lantern (\"Haunted\"); a bench on a little platform under a clock stopped near midnight (\"I Can't Wait\"); a red gate hung with paper lanterns and a phone on a post (\"Kyoto\"); a bare tree still standing in a ring of stones with one lantern (\"Still Standing\"); a pool of gold under the ice with a shovel beside it (\"The Gold\"); a camcorder on a tripod pointed at an empty bench, its red light on (\"On Video\"); a viewing platform with coin binoculars over the lake (\"Panorama\"). Big Thief: a ring of birches hung with red lanterns round a stone table (\"Vampire Empire\"); a beehive on a post in a swarm of glowing motes (\"Simulation Swarm\"); an empty frame glowing at its edges (\"Not\"); a tangle of string lights caught in a bush (\"Incomprehensible\"); one steady candle on a stone in a ring of snow (\"Certainty\"); two lanterns on two posts joined by a glowing thread (\"Real Love\"). Lizzy McAlpine: a kneeler before a candle shrine (\"Pushing It Down and Praying\"), glowing footprints zigzagging through the snow (\"Drunk, Running\"), a table crowded with little things under string lights (\"A Little Bit of Everything\"). Mitski: a kennel with a prize rosette (\"I Bet on Losing Dogs\"), a mailbox stuffed with glowing letters (\"Francis Forever\"). Noah Kahan: a leaning pile of bare sticks with red leaves on the snow (\"Stick Season\"), a signpost of arrows with a suitcase at its foot (\"You're Gonna Go Far\"). Daughter: a little cabinet of glowing bottles (\"Medicine\"), a swing set half buried in snow (\"Youth\").","places":[["the lending library","a little cabin with a stove, two armchairs, shelves of books and a cat to stroke;"],["the greenhouse","glowing warm in the snow, full of flowers, with a stool among the plants and a watering can;"],["the ice lantern garden","a spiral of ice lanterns to light one by one;"],["the hot spring","steaming among stones and lanterns, to sit in while the snow falls;"],["the star deck","two long chairs and a brass telescope for watching the sky."]]},"The Shoegaze Fens":{"arriving":"You arrive inside a ruined cathedral at dusk, half sunk in a wide shallow fen. Its roof is gone except for a few stone ribs against a pink sky; tall pointed windows of pink, violet and blue stained glass glow from inside, a great rose window burns over the open west door, and one tall broken tower keeps a lit belfry above the fog. Down the nave runs a long still pool lined with candles, past rows of columns (two fallen) to a glowing orb over the altar. Outside the door the hills fall away into still, glowing water, and every artist has a little island of moss in it.","vibe":"Dreamy, loud, a little cheesy and a little heavy: a warm, saturated dusk, never grey (unlike the Driftwood Shore's cold morning). Permanent soft fog, heavy bloom, dusty pink, lavender haze and fen green. You wade knee-deep between the islands or cross on old stone causeways and lines of giant guitar pedals laid as stepping stones, their little lights still on. Lily pads with glowing flowers, broken pillars standing in the shallows with candles burning on them, amps half sunk with their lights still on, weeping willows along the banks, reeds round every island. Petals drift down all the time and motes of light rise off the water; rings of light spread across the water from the islands, faster and brighter as a song swells, and the cathedral glass breathes with the music. The light turns warm rose for hopeful songs and deep violet for sad ones. Trails climb into the hills round the fen to things worth finding: a ruined bell tower, a stone circle with a glowing heart, a moon gate, a great willow hung with lanterns; on the way, willows, flowering shrubs, standing stones, benches with lamps facing the water and a fallen speaker overgrown with flowers. When Slowdive's \"When the Sun Hits\" plays, the sun breaks through: shafts of gold pour into the fen, the fog and the petals turn gold, and Slowdive's sun blazes, for as long as it plays. Now and then a heron flies low over the water, the cathedral bell rings rings of light out over the fen as a song begins, loud songs bring a storm of petals, a little fleet of floating candles drifts across the water, and a heron stands somewhere new in the shallows every day.","roads":"Out in the open water near their islands, Deafheaven's, Mazzy Star's, Slowdive's and my bloody valentine's songs are dreamy, floating places, each reached by wading along a line of floating candles on lily pads to a sign on a post, each glowing (and moving a little more) while its song plays. Deafheaven: a little black house with warm windows adrift on a raft of light, bobbing on the water (\"Dream House\"); a ring of twelve glass pillars, every one its own colour (\"Great Mass of Color\"); a stair climbing out of the water to a glowing door standing in mid-air (\"Vertigo\"). Mazzy Star: a broken stone arch with a lantern at its peak looking down at its own reflection (\"Look On Down From The Bridge\"); a frosted pier with ice floes round it and snow hanging in the air, turning slowly (\"Quiet, The Winter Harbor\"); a thicket of glowing red roses with petals circling on the water (\"Roseblood\"). Slowdive: a great golden ring standing in the water (\"When the Sun Hits\"); a stone chair on a plinth with paper lanterns circling round it (\"Alison\"). my bloody valentine: a crescent moon rocking on the water like a cradle, with a pillow and a blanket (\"When You Sleep\"); three terraced pools of pink light (\"Only Shallow\").","places":[["The Pedalboard","on the east hill: five effects pedals the size of small cars on a dark board, a row of tall lights behind. Stomp a footswitch and the lights take the effect: chorus drifts and doubles them, reverb swells them slowly, fuzz frays them, delay sends a pulse down the row, tremolo shivers them. All five can be on at once."],["The Swing at the End of the World","a tall timber frame on the last rise before the sea, two swings on long ropes to sit on or push, a lamp at each foot."],["The Glasshouse","on the north headland: a ruined white iron conservatory, its surviving panes glowing pink, violet and blue; giant glowing flowers down a tiled path, a dry fountain, benches, a gardener, a watering can."],["The Last Dance","in the south hollow: a floor of pink and violet tiles with a slow light moving across it, a mirror ball hung from a broken stone arch (pull the cord to spin it), two couples who never stopped, a chair for sitting this one out."]]},"The Driftwood Shore":{"arriving":"The morning after a storm on a cold shore. Silver sea, silver sky, pearl mist, and a thin line of rose and gold where the light is trying to break. Low cloud races in off the sea on a strong wind, waves break on the sand, and salt mist blows across the dunes. You arrive at the heart of it: a still lagoon ringed by a low boardwalk, a little footbridge across it, and a curved arch of bleached driftwood rising out of the water over the bridge, lashed with rope, sea-glass floats and a ship's bell hanging from it and a lantern burning at each foot. The artists are spread round the lagoon; from there a long promenade with a signpost marked \"the sea\" runs out across the dunes to the beach.","vibe":"Bittersweet. The colours come from its covers: silver-grey and rose, ember, pencil-grey. The storm has left everything strewn along the tide line: bleached logs and branches, seaweed, shells, buoys, lost crab pots, sea glass glinting in aqua, green and amber, tide pools with starfish, and a wreck half buried in the sand with its broken mast still up. The warmth is in small things: bonfires still smouldering with a thermos left on a log, a row of faded beach huts with a couple of windows lit, lanterns along the low grey boardwalk (rope rails you can lean on), a lifeguard tower where the promenade meets the sand, the bones of a whale on the beach, and a beacon at the end of a jetty running straight out into open water, sweeping its beam through the mist. Beyond the boardwalk the dunes and heath are wild but never empty: wind-bent trees leaning away from the sea, sand fences half buried, cairns with a piece of sea glass glowing on top, fishing nets drying on poles, stacks of old crab pots, lone benches with a lantern facing the view, upturned boats made into sheds with a lit window, and a roofless stone cottage with a lamp still burning on the hearth. Silvery marram grass bends in the wind on the dunes; grey-green heath and pink sea thrift inland. The light breaks rose and gold for hopeful songs and turns cold silver for sad ones; some sad songs bring a passing drizzle. When \"Somewhere Over the Rainbow\" plays, a rainbow rises out toward the sea, seen through the driftwood arch, and stays while it plays. It is never quite the same twice: gulls always wheel over the shore and in some songs a big flock comes in off the sea; an old sailing ship sometimes crosses the horizon with a lantern lit at its stern; now and then a whale spouts three times out at sea and lifts its tail; lightning flickers far out over the water in the drizzly songs; in some hopeful songs the cloud breaks and shafts of light fall on the sea; and every day a message in a bottle washes up somewhere different, glowing faintly green. All of it is the same for friends standing together.","roads":"Laid out on purpose, with the lagoon, the promenade and every artist left where they were. Five more boardwalks, the same grey deck and lanterns, run out to the empty north-east headland, the east heath, the south-west shore and the south. At their ends, with the people of the shore to talk to:","places":[["The Lookout","on the north-east headland: a white coastguard hut with a lit window, a mast of signal flags, a brass telescope, the night's log to read, a bench to the sea."],["The Smokehouse","three tall tarred net huts, the middle one glowing at its door; fish drying on racks, herring and kippers to carry, crates to sit on, someone mending a net the storm tore."],["The Boatyard","a hull in frame on a slip, pale ribs and half its planking; a workbench of tools and shavings, a finished dinghy on the grass to sit in, a launching bell to ring."],["The Beachcomber's","by the south-west shore: a driftwood-and-tin shack hung with floats and glowing sea glass, a table of sea glass sorted by colour (every piece can be taken), a driftwood horse and heron."],["The Sauna","a little cedar hut with light under its door and a steaming barrel tub on a deck, out of the wind; sit in the tub."]]},"The Bedroom Pop Meadows":{"arriving":"A deep lilac field just past sunset: the sun has gone, the sky is violet with a pink glow low down, and fireflies are coming out over the grass. In the middle lies a giant unmade bed, its duvet spilling down to the grass so you can walk up onto it, with huge pillows, a pair of giant headphones, and a bedside lamp that lights the whole field.","vibe":"The daydream half of bedroom music (the Small Hours is the other half: the making). This is the music you pace your room to for hours, so the land's spine is a track: a loop you can walk round and round, past every artist, lined both sides with fairy lights. A pulse of light chases round them, quicker on energetic songs, slower on quiet ones, with a dash of light at every new song. Quiet songs bring out more fireflies and fade glow-in-the-dark star stickers into the sky, like the ones on a bedroom ceiling; happy songs send soap bubbles up from the field and turn the light pinker. Deep lilac grass, purple clover, pastel flowers and dandelion clocks; trees hung with fairy lights. Bedroom things dreamed out into the field: glowing lava lamps, giant polaroids of the land's own covers, paper planes stuck nose-first in the grass, puffy star cushions, fairy lights on tall poles, and pastel paper lanterns rising slowly into the sky. A staircase of giant books leads up onto the bed.","roads":"Clairo's and beabadoobee's songs are little places out in the meadow, each on its own lilac path with a painted sign, glowing while its song plays. Clairo: packed bags waiting by a door standing alone in the grass (\"Bags\"), a vanity mirror ringed with bulbs (\"Pretty Girl\"), a chalk heart drawn on the grass with a tape player in it (\"Sofia\"). beabadoobee: a giant steaming coffee mug (\"Coffee\"), a giant glue bottle with paper hearts stuck round it (\"Glue Song\"), two swings hung facing away from each other (\"Estranged\"). Every one-song artist has a piece from their song beside their daydream: Anna Sofia's thorny little globe, Balu Brigada's car backseat, Beach Bunny's tiara, Cavetown's cardboard town with one red window, Chaos Chaos's neon heart, Dazy's fuzzy creature, Foster The People's drum pads, London Grammar's candle in a jar, Loving's slow wave, Monsune's speech bubble, Pacific Soul's hibiscus, Peach Pit's peach split round a glowing pit, Royel Otis's candles, The Apples In Stereo's apples and lightning bolt, The Neighbourhood's sweater on a hanger in falling snow, The Postal Service's mailbox and paper plane, fun.'s crescent of little lights and stars.","places":[["The Record Player","a turntable the size of a room, the record turning slowly; sit on the record and go round with it. A speed switch (33 or 45), two speakers breathing light, cushions."],["The Window Seat","a piece of bedroom wall standing in the grass with a big window in it, the night and a moon in the window, curtains, glow-stars on the wallpaper; a deep cushioned sill to curl up on, a lamp, a cat to stroke."],["The Bubble Bath","a giant clawfoot tub on gold feet, heaped with bubbles that drift up into the sky; steps up, places to sit in the bubbles, a giant duck bobbing, a small rubber duck to carry."],["The Sleepover","sleeping bags like petals round a torch standing on end under fairy lights; hold the torch under your chin and the light turns ghost-story green. Pillows to throw, popcorn to carry."],["The Notebook","open flat on the grass and big enough to walk on, doodles glowing on one page and a to-do list on the other; a giant pencil to sit on, a giant mug of cocoa, a tape player whose reels turn when you press play."]]},"Garage Rock Row":{"arriving":"A gritty small-town main street late at night after rain. You arrive at one end of the Row by a zebra crossing: wet asphalt shining with neon and puddles, orange sodium street lamps, muscle cars at the kerb, and the street running away to a neon casino at the far end.","vibe":"Loud, late and a bit rough, nothing like the Small Hours' sleeping hamlet. Down both sides: a garage for every artist, door rolled up, the band's gear set up inside under string lights, their name in neon over the door; between them a pub, a diner, a gas station, a laundromat, a record shop, a bar, a pawn shop and a motel, each with a neon sign, and back alleys with dumpsters, burn barrels and steam. The casino closes off the street: marquee bulbs chasing, gold doors, a sign tower with a star. Behind the power lines the town gives way to the suburbs: a quiet grid of residential streets running parallel to the Row and crossed every block, lined with small nondescript houses (gable roofs, porch lights, mailboxes, driveways, a car here, a tree there). Most windows are dark at this hour, some lit warm, a few flickering blue with a TV, and now and then a garage stands with its door up and a band's gear inside under a bare bulb, the glow spilling onto the driveway. Beyond the suburbs, dark hills with a water tower, lit billboards, a junkyard, a radio mast blinking red, power lines and chain-link fences. Cars cruise up and down the Row with their headlights on (the same cars for everyone), sometimes a police car with its lights going; some sad songs bring drizzle. \"Sex on Fire\" sets every burn barrel roaring; \"505\" floods the street red and room 505's door pulses; \"When You Were Young\" sends searchlights sweeping from the casino and the marquee goes wild.","roads":"On lots along the suburban streets, each under a tall neon sign with the song's name that you can see over the rooftops, each glowing and pulsing while its song plays. Arctic Monkeys: a red phone booth with the receiver hanging (\"Do I Wanna Know?\"), a motorbike under a giant cyan neon question mark (\"R U Mine?\"). Cage the Elephant: a car on an overlook with its headlights on toward the town (\"Cigarette Daydreams\"), a busker's pallet stage with a mic, an amp, a spotlight and an open guitar case catching coins (\"Ain't No Rest for the Wicked\"). Silversun Pickups: a stack of old TVs glowing blue (\"Lazy Eye\"), a big red button on a striped pedestal between two warning lights (\"Panic Switch\"). The Kooks: an ice-cream van with a neon cone on the roof (\"Naive\"), a light-up dance floor under a mirror ball (\"She Moves In Her Own Way\"). The Strokes: a corner of a ball field, home plate, a backstop, a dugout bench with a pennant and a stadium light (\"Ode To The Mets\"). The Voidz: an arcade cabinet glitching on a pallet (\"Did My Best\").","places":[["Lucky Strike Lanes","a bowling alley to walk into under a neon sign and a giant pin. Three lanes, thirty pins that really fall, balls on a rack to carry and throw, a red button that stands the pins back up, a shoe counter, a bench."],["The Skate Park","two quarter pipes, a fun box with a rail, a ledge to sit on, neon tags; skateboards to carry, a boombox to press play on, someone who has been about to drop in since nine."],["Shady Acres","the trailer park in the south-west hills: four trailers round a gravel yard under string lights, a fire in an oil drum, lawn chairs to sit in, lawn flamingos, a cooler of cans, a band rehearsing outdoors because of the landlord."]]},"The Electric Fields":{"arriving":"A whimsical music festival on rolling hills at golden dusk, sliding into night. You arrive at the festival gate, scaffold towers wrapped in flags under a light-up sign, with a ring of tall flags, a striped merch tent and a map of the fields round it. Flag-lined walkways of trodden straw, strung with bunting and festoon lights, lead to every stage.","vibe":"Uplifting, upbeat, a little weird. MGMT's Big Top is the heart: a huge red-and-cream striped tent with a mirrored disco ball bursting through its roof, throwing beams; inside, a stage of synths under a light rig. Round the fields: a Ferris wheel turning on the highest hill, campsites of bright tents with little fires and flags, food trucks with lit hatches and picnic tables, hay bales with synths set up on them, giant inflatables (a flower, a smiling sun, a rainbow arch, a mushroom), light towers, a row of portable toilets, glowsticks dropped in the grass. Lasers sweep from the main stage, faster in loud songs; the lights and the LED wall pulse with the music; now and then a short fireworks show, and a hot-air balloon drifting over. \"Kids\" fills the sky over the Big Top with fireworks and confetti for the whole song; \"Electric Feel\" sends electric blue along every string of lights and every light tower, and the neon eel blazes; \"Disparate Youth\" sets off the flame cannons on the main stage; and LCD Soundsystem's dome sits dim and waiting until one of their songs plays, when its mirror ball spins and every light in it comes on: the set that finally happens.","roads":"Out in the fields, festival installations for MGMT's and Santigold's songs, each down its own straw walkway to a flag sign, big and bright enough to read from across a field, each glowing and pulsing while its song plays. MGMT: a pretend mansion front with lit windows and gold doors, propped up from behind by struts, a red carpet and flashbulbs on posts (\"Time to Pretend\"); a giant smiling inflatable skull with neon green eyes and pink teeth, tied down with ropes (\"When You Die\"); two giant hands, one in a blue sleeve and one in a pink, shaking on a plinth with sparks round the clasp (\"The Handshake\"); a frozen pond ringed with snowy pines with deep blue tips, a red sled on the ice (\"Siberian Breaks\"). Santigold: a loop of red running track with a lit finish arch (\"Run the Road\"); a brick wall glowing with neon spray shapes, paint cans at its foot and a street lamp (\"L.E.S. Artistes\").","places":[["The Silent Disco","in the south field: a dark round floor and a ring of dim poles, eight people dancing hard to nothing you can hear. Put the headphones on at the rack and the floor and every light come alive on the beat; take them off and it is a field of people dancing to crickets."],["The Secret Stage","beyond it: hidden in a ring of trees strung with fairy lights, a stage of pallets, one performer, hay bales to sit on. \"you found it\"."],["The Carousel","in the north-east: always turning under a striped canopy; horses, unicorns and giraffes going up and down. Sit on one and ride. A ticket booth (rides free, forever)."],["Lost & Found","in the north: a canvas tent with a table of single things, all of which can be carried off (one left wellington, a phone with 37 missed calls, a cuddly dinosaur)."],["The Chai Tent","in the west: a low patchwork tent full of rugs, cushions to sit on round low tables, cups of chai, lanterns, a gong to strike."]]},"The Small Hours":{"arriving":"3 am, deep indigo, a quiet drizzle. By the way in, an all-night laundrette glows, its machines turning. Wet stone lanes under orange street lamps lead up into a hollow of tiny homes, every window lit.","vibe":"The making half of bedroom music (the Bedroom Pop Meadows next door is the daydream). These are small, newer artists, and every home is somewhere music is being made right now: walk in and there's a desk under a lamp, a laptop, a microphone, plants, and their covers on the wall. Moss, ferns and garden hedges; stars through gaps in the cloud. Between the homes the hollow is a whole sleeping neighbourhood: the neighbours' cottages dark but for the odd landing light, garden sheds with washing left out on the line in the drizzle, wet dry-stone walls with gates hanging open, dark garden trees, a lone street lamp over a bench and a puddle, and a drinks machine humming blue at a corner under telegraph wires.","roads":"Out in the sleeping neighbourhood, each down its own wet lane with a sign and a little lamp, glowing while its song plays. Steph.fm: a night bus stop with a lit timetable and a greyhound waiting by the bench (\"Greyhound\"), a patch of old TV aerials with blinking red tips (\"Antenna Patch\"), a tall mirror standing in a garden with a stool in front (\"a second glance\"). Bell Thompson: a café table with a record turning under a blue neon (\"Always Like This\"), a trophy on a pedestal under a spotlight inside velvet ropes (\"Most Prized\"). Round Noah Gallagher's place: a clearing of snow with snow hanging in the air round a lamp (\"Whiteout\"), a corridor of torch-lit stone posts (\"The Gauntlet\"), a lectern with an open book glowing teal among orchid and green orbs (\"Doctrine Factorum\"). By every one-song artist's door, a piece from their song: Boowah's walnut shell with a light inside, Carmen Silver's motes hanging in the air, Fionavair's old TV on the step, Hailey Picardi's glowing scribble, John Luther's pot of glowing leaves, Josie on the Rocks' gate with a NOT YOU sign, Sōra's puddle holding the streetlight, runfromobm's toastie on a garden table. The places stay outside the radio stations' rings.","places":[["The Night Café","one long lit window, the brightest thing in the hollow; booths, a counter with stools, tea in mugs you can carry, a pie under glass, a radio turned very low."],["The Corner Shop","open-fronted and cold-lit under a striped awning; shelves of bright things, tomorrow's paper on the rack to read, milk, crisps and batteries to carry off."],["The Phone Box","it rings, its light pulsing, until you answer; then it is quiet for a while."],["The Playground","empty at this hour: two swings to push or sit on, a roundabout to spin and ride, a slide, a see-saw, a bench under the one lamp."]]},"The Country Plains":{"arriving":"The Square in the little town of Wheatley, at golden hour. A water tower on four legs with the town's name across the tank, a flagpole, a green with benches round it, a gas pump and a pickup, a horse at a hitching rail, a lemonade stand with glasses to carry. The sun is low and gold, the shadows long, and the evening is coming on slow.","vibe":"Easy, warm, a little bit wistful. Wheat stands gold in fields behind split-rail fences all the way to the edge of the land; red barns with white-trimmed doors, windmills turning, round hay bales to sit on, cottonwoods going gold at the edges, old tractors left where they stopped. Main Street runs out of the Square between false-front stores on little boardwalks: The Rusty Boot honky-tonk with a jukebox out front, the general store, the diner, the feed and seed, the post office, a little white church with a steeple. At the far end is the Bonfire Field: pickups parked in a ring with their tailgates down, string lights from pole to pole, hay bales and camp chairs round a big fire, and a guitar going round.","happenings":"Windmills turn; the wheat sways; the bonfire throws sparks; fireflies come up out of the grass as it gets dark. \"Something in the Orange\" turns the sunset deep orange. \"American Honey\" is honey gold. \"Big Green Tractor\" is green and gold. \"Colder Weather\" brings snow. \"Rain Is A Good Thing\" brings rain. \"Need You Now\" and \"Walkin' After Midnight\" bring the night. \"The Night The Lights Went Out In Georgia\" puts every lamp out. \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" turns the light red and makes the bonfire roar. \"Can't Fight The Moonlight\" is silver moonlight. \"Hurt\" drains the colour.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Square, Main Street and the Bonfire Field are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Square","take a lemonade; sit on the green."],["Main Street","drop a quarter in the jukebox at The Rusty Boot."],["The Bonfire Field","sit on a tailgate or a hay bale; play the guitar by the fire."],["The prairie","climb on an old tractor; sit on a hay bale and watch the windmill."]]},"The Pop Republic":{"arriving":"The Plaza, on a bright day under a deep blue sky. A great white fountain throwing up spray, billboards all round (SING IT!, ENCORE, SOLD OUT), confetti cannons, strings of pink, lemon and cyan bulbs, a giant gold photo frame to strike a pose in, an ice-cream cart. Straight ahead the Red Carpet runs to the Palace, and behind the Palace a ferris wheel turns.","vibe":"Joyful, everybody singing along, a big show. White paving and candy-coloured townhouses with striped awnings; lollipop trees on the hills; fountains; billboards; benches under star-topped lamps; confetti in the air. The Red Carpet runs between velvet ropes on gold posts, with a star in the pavement for every artist and photographers flashing away behind the ropes. At its end the Palace: a white stage-palace with pink domes, an arch of bulbs, a crowd at the barrier and a microphone anybody can take. Off to one side, K-Pop Square: an idol stage with a screen of light and six sweeping beams, a table of light sticks, fans doing the choreography.","happenings":"The ferris wheel turns; the fountains spray; confetti drifts over the Plaza and bursts from the cannons whenever anybody plays; photographers' flashes; strings of bulbs chasing; the K-Pop Square beams sweeping. \"Firework\" sets off fireworks over the whole land. \"Cruel Summer\" is a pink-orange sunset. \"Lavender Haze\" lays a lavender haze. \"Red\" and \"All Too Well\" turn the light red and drop red leaves. \"Blank Space\" turns everything white. \"Thriller\" brings a green night. \"Dynamite\" is disco colours and confetti. \"Skyfall\" darkens the sky and brings gold down. \"Snow Angel\" brings snow. \"Supernova\" flashes white. \"Roar\" is gold, \"Juno\" pink, and the K-pop songs light up the square.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Plaza, the Red Carpet and the Palace are the spine, with a lane to K-Pop Square; every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Plaza","strike a pose in the gold frame, take an ice cream, sit by the fountain."],["The Red Carpet","find your favourite's star; wave at the photographers."],["The Palace","climb the steps and take the mic; watch the ferris wheel turn behind you."],["K-Pop Square","take a light stick, do the choreography."]]},"The Heartbreak Boardwalk":{"arriving":"The Promenade, at dusk. A circle of weathered boards round an old white bandstand with a striped roof and one lamp lit, benches turned to face the sunset, a payphone, a fortune-teller machine, a shuttered stall. The sun sits low and pink over the sea and the sky goes violet above it. The Boardwalk runs from here all the way down to the water and out along the Pier.","vibe":"Sad and glamorous, the end of the season. Faded clapboard beach houses on short stilts with porch swings, one window lit; palms leaning toward the sea; dune grass and dune fences; globe lamps on curly iron posts coming on one by one. Down the Boardwalk: shuttered stalls (SALTWATER TAFFY, FORTUNES, PRIZES), a carousel standing still, a wooden rollercoaster with a single car left at the top. The Heartbreak Motel has half its sign lit, VACANCY in pink neon, and an empty pool. Out on the Pier: people fishing, a telescope, and the Last Chance Café with its light still on.","happenings":"The neon buzzes and drops out; the lamps breathe; gulls glide over; the sea glimmers; the carousel lights up when anybody plays. \"Summertime Sadness\" brings a hot gold haze. \"Ultraviolence\" turns it deep violet. \"West Coast\" is pink and gold. \"ocean eyes\" turns everything sea-blue and lights the water. \"Happiness is a butterfly\" brings butterflies. \"Lasso The Moon\" is cool silver moonlight. \"good 4 u\" flickers purple. \"Happier Than Ever\" gathers a storm and brings the rain. \"No Time To Die\" goes dark. \"Counting Stars\" brings the night. \"Stop The Wedding!\" scatters white petals.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Promenade, the Boardwalk and the Pier are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Promenade","sit in the bandstand, make a call from the payphone, ask the machine for your fortune."],["The Boardwalk","sit on a carousel horse; look up at the rollercoaster."],["The Heartbreak Motel","lie back by the empty pool."],["The Pier","look through the telescope; get a table by the rail at the Last Chance Café."]]},"The Glitterlands":{"arriving":"Under the Mirrorball, at dusk. A disco ball as big as a house turns high over a floor of glowing tiles, hung by chains from four light pillars, throwing specks of light everywhere. Round the floor: banks of pink and lilac cloud, a glitter bar with drinks to carry, a photo booth, people dancing. The sky is lilac with a pink horizon and glitter is always drifting down.","vibe":"Dancing on your own and dancing with everyone. The ground is a soft land of lilac and pink cloud. Little light-up dance floors sit on it with a mirror ball over each, and more float up in the air on their own clouds; crystal arches, bunches of heart balloons, velvet sofas under mirror-ball lamps. The Glitter Mile runs from the Mirrorball as a runway of glowing tiles under arches of light, to the Sky Club: a round dance floor raised on cloud, a wall of light, a DJ booth with records to drop, and a staircase of light up to a floating balcony with its own little floor.","happenings":"The Mirrorball turns and throws light; every floor pulses four to the floor and shifts colour; glitter falls; confetti over the Sky Club when anybody plays. \"Run Away With Me\" turns the light pink. \"Dragostea din tei\" and \"Caramelldansen\" make every floor flash fast in every colour. \"Happy Nation\" is gold. \"What Is Love\" pulses slow red. \"Red Wine Supernova\" goes red-violet with bursts of stars. \"Supercut\" flickers like film. \"Mercurial World\" turns everything silver. \"Good Time\" brings confetti everywhere. \"Image\" is electric blue.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Mirrorball, the Glitter Mile and the Sky Club are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Mirrorball","dance on the floor of light; take a drink from the glitter bar; take some photos."],["The Glitter Mile","strut it."],["The Sky Club","drop a record; climb the staircase of light to the floating balcony."]]},"The Latin Coast":{"arriving":"La Plaza, at sunset. A jacaranda in full purple flower in the middle of the square, wrapped in fairy lights, with a white tiled bench round its trunk; papel picado strung from the tree out to the lanterns; a tiled dance floor full of people dancing; a mariachi band on a little striped stage; an aguas frescas stall with cups to carry; and the church, white with a yellow bell tower and a bell you can ring.","vibe":"Warm, loud and joyful, a party that started hours ago. Houses painted every colour (pink, marigold, turquoise, cobalt, lime) with white parapets, shutters, balconies spilling flowers, a lamp by every door. Calle Mayor runs down the hill over warm cobbles under lines of papel picado, past wrought-iron lanterns, to the Harbour: a stone quay out over the water with painted fishing boats moored either side, a red-and-white striped lighthouse at the end, nets drying, and a mariscos stand. On the hills: palms, bougainvillea and jacaranda, agaves, tiled benches under lanterns, fruit carts under striped awnings.","happenings":"Lanterns glow; the lighthouse beam sweeps round; jacaranda petals drift down in the plaza; pigeons burst up from the bell tower whenever anybody rings it. \"Hips Don't Lie\" turns the light hot gold. \"Whenever, Wherever\" gets the wind up. \"Danza Kuduro\" and \"La Bamba\" bring confetti and party colours. \"Macarena\" flashes pink on every beat. \"Havana\" is a hot amber haze. \"Mar De Soledad\" is a lonely blue dusk. \"Tití Me Preguntó\" is pink. \"Renacer\" is a new dawn. \"Ella Baila Sola\" dims everything. \"La Cucaracha\" is marigold.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: La Plaza, Calle Mayor and the Harbour are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["La Plaza","sit under the jacaranda, dance, shout a grito at the mariachis, ring the church bell, take an agua fresca."],["Calle Mayor","the painted houses and the papel picado."],["The Harbour","walk out along the quay to the lighthouse; eat ceviche by the boats."]]},"The Synthwave Coast":{"arriving":"The Neon Diner, at night. A long chrome diner with a band of pink-lit windows and a sign on a tall pole, chrome sports cars parked nose-in out front, a glowing magenta grid laid over the lot, neon palms and cyan tube lamps round the edge. Inside: a counter with stools, booths along the window, milkshakes to carry, an arcade cabinet. Straight ahead the Coast Road runs toward a giant striped sun sitting on the sea.","vibe":"A night drive with the windows down, a little nostalgic for a time you never had. The sky is deep violet with a magenta band on the horizon and stars overhead. The ground is dark, with squares of glowing grid laid over it here and there; palms with neon bands round their trunks, wireframe pyramids glowing pink and cyan, black billboards edged in neon (NIGHT DRIVE, 1986, TURBO), chrome cars parked at odd angles, benches under tube lamps. The Coast Road is a dark road with pink edge lines and cyan dashes and a pink car cruising up and down it. It ends at Sunset Point on the cliff: a lookout with a chrome rail, a neon sign, two cars facing the sun, and the sun itself out over the sea.","happenings":"The striped sun breathes on the horizon; the grid pulses to a slow synth beat; the neon hums; a pink car cruises the Coast Road; shooting stars now and then. \"Days of Thunder\" brings lightning over the sea and rain. \"Vampires\" turns everything blood red. \"Neon Odyssey\" flares the neon cyan. \"Arcade Summer\" is a hot orange sunset. \"Genesee Avenue\" is a dark red horror glow. \"A Moment Apart\" brings shooting stars down everywhere. \"Feel It All Around\" is a warm hazy gold. \"RYDEEN\" sets the colours racing. \"Always\" is soft pink.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Neon Diner, the Coast Road and Sunset Point are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Neon Diner","sit at the counter or in a booth; take a milkshake; play the arcade game."],["The Coast Road","walk it toward the sun; sit in a parked car."],["Sunset Point","sit in a car facing the sun at the end of the road."]]},"The Soul Lowlands":{"arriving":"The town square of a wooden river town at golden hour: brick underfoot, a magnolia in a round planter with benches round it, globe lamps strung together with bulbs, the town clock. On one side stands The Royal, a theatre with a tall blade sign and a marquee of chasing bulbs; beside it a white chapel with its doors open and the choir inside. Main Street runs away east toward the river.","vibe":"Warm and joyful. Long shadows and honey light under a blue sky, gold motes hanging in the low sun. Main Street runs down from the square between pastel shopfronts with striped awnings, under strings of bulbs that chase slowly: a record shop, a barbershop with a turning pole, a hotel, a hat shop, a bakery, a luncheonette, a radio station on the air, a pool hall, a tailor, an ice cream parlour. Beyond the shops River Road carries on under spreading oaks to the landing, where a white paddle steamer is moored with her red wheel turning. Out in the meadows: oaks and magnolias, pastel houses with porch swings and lit windows behind picket fences, brick-red lanes with globe lamps, tall grass going gold, wildflowers. Everyone is in their Sunday best, and somebody is always singing.","happenings":"The bulbs chase with the music; the paddle wheel turns; the barber pole spins. \"Sir Duke\" sends the bulbs racing and notes rising from the brass. \"Superstition\" makes the lamps flicker and turns the light violet. \"(Sittin' On) the Dock of the Bay\" softens the light to rose and brings gulls over the dock. \"Stand By Me\" brings the night, and the moon is the only light. \"Oh Happy Day\" sets the whole land blazing gold. \"Georgia on My Mind\" sends peach blossom drifting from the tree.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the square, Main Street and River Road are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them. The people of the town will talk to you; each place has somewhere to sit, something to use and something to carry.","places":[["The Royal","a theatre to walk into: a stage with a velvet curtain, a microphone to step up to (the spotlight finds you), a pianist, rows of velvet seats. A ticket seller outside who won't take your money."],["The Chapel","white, with a steeple and tall coloured windows; pews to sit in, the choir in robes on their risers, an organ to play (the windows blaze), paper fans to carry."],["Sweet Soul Records","bins of records down every wall, a turntable to drop the needle on, records to carry off (and throw)."],["The Luncheonette","a counter with stools, two booths, a jukebox, a cook at the grill, pie and coffee to carry."],["The Deluxe Barbershop","two chairs to sit in, a wall of mirrors, the barber, a turning pole outside."],["W-S-O-L Radio","a mast with a red light, an ON AIR sign, and a horn speaker on the wall to listen at."],["The Corner","three singers under a lamp on Main Street who need a fourth."],["The Landing and the Lowlands Belle","a boardwalk over the water with lamps and benches, and the riverboat alongside: walk aboard, pull the whistle, sit in a deck chair at the bow."]]},"The Fusion Isthmus":{"arriving":"A small brick square at blue hour, just after sunset: the sky deep teal, brass lamps lit, a brass star set in the paving, a skate ledge with a brass edge, pots of plants. Brass Lane leads north to the canal. (The plan called this a strip of land between two seas; the map has no such seas, so the water here is a canal.)","vibe":"Smooth and warm, and in no hurry. A teal canal runs straight through the town between two quays, strings of lights across the water, iron railings, brass lamps. Tall narrow brick houses line both quays, painted brick-red, teal, indigo and cream, their windows lit, a box of flowers under every one, a bicycle by a door. The Piano Bridge crosses in the middle, its deck laid out as keys that light up as you walk over. Beside it the Rooftop, the town's club, is up an outside stair on the flat roof of the tallest house. A houseboat is moored below; a little rowing boat with a lantern drifts the length of the canal and back. Out past the houses: plane trees with a few lights in their branches, brick planters, benches, buskers on the corners.","happenings":"The strings of lights twinkle in turn and lift when anybody plays; the canal shimmers; the Piano Bridge lights under your feet; the rowing boat drifts. \"Tidal Wave\" brightens the canal and sets it rippling. \"Nightrider\" dims the town and turns the lamps up. \"Turquoise Galaxy\" turns the sky turquoise. \"Victory Dance\" throws confetti over Ezra Collective's party. \"Strings of Light\" makes every string of bulbs in the town blaze.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the square, Brass Lane, the two quays and the Piano Bridge are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them. The people here will talk to you, slowly.","places":[["The Rooftop","up the outside stair: a trio short of a drummer (so sit in), a bar with drinks to carry, three tables with chairs that don't match, strings of lights, the view down the canal."],["What Kinda Coffee","a café to walk into: an espresso machine and a barista, a beat machine to tap, a crate of records, coffee and a croissant to carry, and Rhodes the cat on the windowsill."],["Potted","a plant shop spilling onto the quay, with plants to carry off."],["The Root Note","the houseboat. Walk aboard down the gangplank; there is a deck chair."],["The square","the skate ledge, boards to carry, a skater who almost lands it."],["The buskers","on corners out of town, each with an open case and something to say."]]},"The Old Jazz Quarter":{"arriving":"Cobble Square, a little past midnight, in the rain. The whole town is in black and white, like an old photograph of a jazz club: wet grey cobbles, tall dark houses, pools of lamplight, steam rising from a grating. A clock on a post has stopped at five past twelve. On one side of the square a red neon marquee says THE BLUE MODE: the only red in town.","vibe":"Late night and smoky, quiet and close, a little sad. It always rains. Colour is kept for the music: the red of the club's sign and the blue light inside it, the brass of the horns and the street lamps, and gold at the top of the hill. Lamp Street climbs from the square over a stone bridge, where somebody is always playing a saxophone to the river, and on up between tall houses (a few windows lit behind their blinds, fire escapes, water tanks on the roofs, HOTEL and BAR in white neon) to the hilltop, where a round white temple with a gold dome stands above the rain. Out of town: bare black trees, puddles, lamps with a bench under them, phone boxes that are worth answering, long black cars parked with their lamps left on.","happenings":"The rain never stops, except for Alice Coltrane: while one of her songs plays the rain stops, gold comes into the sky and the stars come out over the hill. \"What a Wonderful World\" stops the rain as well, and the garden glows. \"Astral Traveling\" brings the stars out too. During \"Strange Fruit\" every lamp, window and sign in town goes down low and one light is left. \"So What\" turns the town blue. \"Take the 'A' Train\" sets the train's windows flashing. \"In the Mood\" sends the marquee bulbs chasing. \"'Tis Autumn\" brings the amber leaves down. \"Blue Rondo à la Turk\" lights the nine stones in twos and threes. The marquee flickers now and then; notes drift up from the saxophone on the bridge; smoke hangs in the club.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the square, Lamp Street, the bridge and the hilltop are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them without crossing the river. The people here are in black and white too, and will talk to you.","places":[["The Blue Mode","the club on the square. Walk in under the marquee: sit at a table, play two chords on the piano, take a drink from the bar."],["Cobble Square","the stopped clock, a news stand with the late edition and umbrellas to carry, a phone box, a long black car at the kerb."],["The bridge","stone, with a lamp at each corner and the saxophonist leaning on the parapet. Stop and listen."],["Lamp Street","the houses up the hill, and people waiting in doorways."],["The temple","above the rain. The view back down over the town."]]},"The Funk Tropics":{"arriving":"A clearing in a neon jungle at night, ringed with lights, a big \"1\" set in the ground. A path of light-up paving, the One, runs straight ahead to the Mothership: a flying saucer landed in the jungle on three legs, lights racing round her rim, her ramp let down onto a stage. Behind you the One becomes Funk Street.","vibe":"Dark, humid and saturated, and three kinds of funk in it. The cosmic, silly kind at the Mothership: a synth to hit, a light-up dance floor, the band's people (one of them from further away than the rest). The cool, sly kind on Funk Street: a short strip of neon half swallowed by the jungle, with a club, a rib shack, shopfronts outlined in light, a long low purple car at the kerb, and a roller rink at the end. And the groove everywhere: glowing purple, lime and orange plants that pulse together on the beat, palms hung with mirror-ball fruit, vines strung between trunks like bass strings, giant platform shoes left in the undergrowth, old speaker stacks the jungle has grown over. Everything that lights, lights in colour.","happenings":"The plants pulse on a shared beat; the paving chases; the Mothership hums and turns slowly on her legs; the car hops. \"Flash Light\" sends beams sweeping the sky from the ship and the giant flashlight. \"Give Up The Funk\" lifts the ship off her legs and floats the torn-off roof higher. \"Purple Rain\" makes it rain purple. \"Maggot Brain\" turns the whole jungle dark but for one spotlight on one guitar. \"Fire on the Bayou\" makes the fire on the pond climb. \"The Payback\" turns the light red.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the One and Funk Street are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them, edged with glowing studs and lit by mirror-ball fruit. The people here strut, and will tell you about it.","places":[["The Mothership","the saucer on her legs, a stage under her ramp with a synth to hit (the rim lights race), a drum kit and amps, a light-up dance floor in front, logs to watch from."],["The Pocket","a club to walk into: a low stage with a bass to slap, a dance floor under a mirror ball, a bar with stools, glowing drinks to carry, a booth."],["Funk Street","shopfronts outlined in neon (menswear with hats to carry off, a guitar shop, a record shop, a chicken place), a rib shack with plates of ribs, and the long low car: sit in it and hit the switches."],["Rollerama","an open-air roller rink under arches of light and a mirror ball, skaters going round, skates to carry (one at a time)."],["The bass vines","here and there in the jungle, four vines strung taut between two trunks. Pluck them."],["The giant shoes","platform shoes the size of a bench, to sit on."]]},"The Far Shores":{"arriving":"The Compass, on a bright blue afternoon: a pale stone plaza with a compass rose in black, white, teal and coral set into it, and a signpost of far-off cities with the minutes to walk to each. A trolley of suitcases, a rack of postcards, an ice-cream cart. West, a wide walk of black and white waves runs downhill between two rows of palms to a teal sea. East, the green gives out and golden dunes begin.","vibe":"Easy, sunny happiness, and the feeling of travelling: a coast of many countries where every stop is somewhere else. The Promenade has a port of call for each artist on either side, with striped canvas roofs, globe lamps, benches, a coconut stand and a kite seller. At the bottom is a white beach with umbrellas and loungers, a sandcastle, surfboards stuck in the sand, and a round thatched beach bar. Behind the Compass the Caravan Track goes off into the dunes, past flags, cairns, a well and three resting camels, to a camp of dark tents round a fire. White clouds, a sea breeze, butterflies. It is the only blue sky in the Groovelands.","happenings":"\"Águas de Março\" brings a warm shower across the land. Mulatu Astatke's \"Tezeta\" turns the whole day the colour of an old photograph. \"Sleep Walk\" dims the afternoon to a lilac dusk and the globe lamps come up. Tinariwen's songs raise the wind, blow sand across the dunes and make the fire leap. \"Banana Boat (Day-O)\" brightens everything: daylight come. \"Estrelar\" sends gold sparkles up round Marcos Valle's. \"Mas, Que Nada!\" rains petals on the beach bar.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Compass is the middle, the Promenade goes west to the beach and the Caravan Track east to the camp, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Compass","read a postcard, take a suitcase or an ice cream, ask the guide."],["The Promenade","the waves underfoot, the coconut stand, the kites."],["The beach","lie on a lounger, add to the sandcastle, take the beach ball."],["The Caravan Track","follow the flags; draw a bucket at the well; greet the camels."]]},"The Delta Crossroads":{"arriving":"Where two dirt roads cross in flat cotton country, on a hot, white, dusty afternoon. A four-armed signpost: THE RAILROAD, DOWN SOUTH, THE RIVER, THE FIELDS. A rusting pickup on one corner, an old man on a crate on another. Out west a storm is stacked up dark on the horizon, flickering, and it does not come. A long shadow of a man in a hat lies down the west road, and nobody is casting it.","vibe":"Raw and haunted. Full daylight and nowhere to hide in it: pale dust, dry grass, cotton in white rows, telegraph poles marching up the Highway with crows on the wires. Scarecrows stand in the fields, bottle trees hung with blue glass catch whatever walks at night, and empty shacks lean under rusted tin. The Highway runs north over the railroad and south into the fields; the cross road runs east down to the river landing and west past the graveyard. The only blue in the land is the neon guitar on the juke joint and the glass in the trees.","happenings":"Dust drifts across the roads; the storm in the west flickers with silent lightning now and then; the bottles glint; the windpump turns; the engine idles smoke. When a Robert Johnson song plays, the afternoon goes dark as midnight, the bulbs gutter, pairs of red eyes open low in the cotton, and at the head of the shadow on the west road something tall stands up. \"Smokestack Lightnin'\" brings the lightning close and often and the engine pours smoke. \"Didn't It Rain\" finally breaks the storm over the whole land. \"The Thrill Is Gone\" takes the colour out of the day. \"Boom Boom\" and Muddy Waters' songs make every bulb stomp in time, and so does the stomp board.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the two roads and the railroad are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them. There are few people here, and they talk like it.","places":[["The crossroads","the signpost, the pickup's tailgate to sit on, the old man, the shadow. Stand in the middle if you like."],["The juke joint","on the north-east corner. Go in and play the riff."],["The railroad","the crossing with its crossbucks, a water tower, a handcar to sit on, the engine."],["The landing","a jetty over the water, cotton bales nobody is collecting, the ferryman fishing, and out past him the wreck of the steamboat Evening Star, aground and listing, one window still lit."],["The graveyard","leaning wooden crosses inside a picket fence, a shut white church, a bell to ring."],["The general store","rocking chairs on the porch, cold drinks in the ice chest, checkers on a barrel."]]},"Discotopia":{"arriving":"On the Floor: a great light-up dance floor that is the town square, nine tiles by nine, chasing in gold, white, amber and aqua. Four white arches rise from its corners to a ring overhead and a mirror ball hangs from it. A DJ in a white and gold booth on one side, a white limousine at the kerb on the other, silver globe lamps all round. It is a clear night, full of stars, by the sea.","vibe":"A sparkling, joyful night in a white resort town, everything picked out in silver, gold and white light. Out along the headland the Lighthouse has a giant mirror ball where its lamp should be: it turns, and specks of light sweep over the town, the lawns and the water. The Esplanade, a white promenade with globe lamps and strings of lights, runs out to it past the silly Euro-disco corner. Inland the Belvedere, a raised white terrace up a grand stair, is where the glamour sits. On the cool blue-green lawns: white villas with gold windows and lights along their roofs, glitter trees dotted with lights, mirror balls on plinths, little tables under parasols, white benches.","happenings":"The tiles and strings of lights chase on a shared beat; the mirror balls turn; specks of light sweep past you. \"Dancing Queen\" turns every tile gold, spins the crown and doubles the specks. \"Waterloo\" fires confetti from the cannon. \"The Winner Takes It All\" dims the whole town and drops one cold light on the podium. \"September\" sets off fireworks over the sea by the lighthouse. \"Rasputin\" spins the onion dome and turns the light red and gold.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Floor at the heart, the Esplanade out to the Lighthouse, a white path back to the Belvedere, and every lane to an artist branching from those, with gold studs down the middle and globe lamps. The people here are dressed for it and will tell you so.","places":[["The Floor","the dance floor square. Ask the DJ for one more song (every tile lights), dance among the dancers, sit in the limousine, and help yourself to a feather boa, a tambourine or a pair of star-shaped glasses."],["The Esplanade","white benches facing the sea, a photo booth to strike a pose in, an ice-cream cart with cones to carry."],["The Lighthouse","white with gold bands and the great mirror ball on top. At its foot a lever to throw (the ball spins up and the specks race), a telescope, the keeper."],["The Belvedere","up the grand stair: little tables to sit at, a champagne tower to pour, glasses to carry, a waiter, and the view back over the Floor."]]},"The Afrobeat Coast":{"arriving":"The roundabout on the hilltop at sunset, among bright painted houses. Beside you is the Big Drum, three times your height. A yellow bus waits at the kerb with its door open and a conductor shouting \"Beach! Beach!\". To the west, Market Road runs straight downhill to the sea, and at the end of it a huge orange sun is going into the water under a purple sky.","vibe":"A joyful street party turning into a beach party. Red-earth roads, strings of orange, gold and pink bulbs chasing down the hill, stalls on both sides of Market Road selling printed cloth, fruit, talking drums, grilled suya, cold drinks and records, a yellow bus going up and down, goats who have seen it all. Houses in yellow, turquoise, pink and green with adverts painted on the walls. At the bottom the road runs out onto the sand: three bonfires with logs to sit on, a circle of drummers, painted canoes, a football pitch scratched in the beach, and the sun's road of light across the water.","happenings":"The bulbs chase down the hill, faster with the music, and jump when the Big Drum is hit; the bonfires flicker and throw sparks; smoke rises from the grills; fireflies come out; the bus stops for anyone standing in the road. \"Water No Get Enemy\" makes the Shrine's fountain leap and turns the evening sea-blue. \"Last Last\" sends the bonfires roaring up. \"Essence\" turns the sky rose and warms every window. \"Fantastic Man\" runs every bulb through every colour. \"Rush\" sends the bus tearing up and down. \"Fall\" rains bananas on Davido's stall. \"love nwantiti\" floats hearts up from CKay's.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the roundabout, Market Road and the beach are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them. There are people everywhere and all of them want to talk.","places":[["The roundabout","the Big Drum to beat, the parked bus to sit in, a drummer who will translate."],["The Shrine","go in under the roof and play the organ riff or the congas; it has been the same song since yesterday."],["Market Road","the stalls (tap a drum, take an orange, a stick of suya, a cold malt, a record with no label), the bus, the goats."],["The beach","sit on a log by a bonfire, join the drum circle, sit in a canoe, kick the football about, watch the sun."]]},"The Hip-Hop Boroughs":{"arriving":"The Block, at golden hour, on top of the mesa. A concrete square with a half court and a ball, a brick mural with the land's name on it in gold, a winners' podium with a trophy on the top step, a cypher round a boombox, a purple lowrider. The sun is low and gold and every shadow is long. Straight ahead, at the end of Victory Avenue, stands the Tower, with a boombox the size of a house on its roof.","vibe":"Big, loud, triumphant. A rooftop city on the mesa tops: blocks of brick and concrete with gold in most of the windows, shops under bright awnings, water towers on the roofs, tags on the side walls, billboards that say LOUDER and YOU MADE IT. Pennants cross Victory Avenue in gold, cream and purple; sneakers hang from the wires; there is a fire hydrant on every corner and a few of them are gold. Street trees gone gold in the light, parked cars with gold rims, dry gold scrub where the paving stops. At the end of the avenue the Stage: a deck under a truss of lights, speaker stacks, a crowd at the barrier, and a microphone anybody can walk up and take.","happenings":"The boombox on the Tower beats, and every speaker with it; the Stage's lights chase; gold dust hangs in the low sun. Take the podium, the Stage's microphone or the cypher's boombox and confetti comes down. \"Not Like Us\" makes the cones pound and drops confetti over everything. \"Alright\" turns the light warmer and gold. \"HUMBLE.\" drains the colour to stark white. \"Lose Yourself\" drops the light and pulses it like a heartbeat. \"Runaway\" goes cool and white. Kid Cudi's songs bring the night down, the only time it comes. \"Fight The Power\" flashes red on the beat. \"SICKO MODE\" switches the light, three times. \"EARFQUAKE\" turns it pink and gets the wind up. \"I Gotta Feeling\" is confetti and party colours. \"Starships\" sends pink sparks up. \"Paper Planes\" sends paper planes over.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Block, Victory Avenue and the Stage are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Block","shoot a basket, take the podium and the trophy, press play at the cypher."],["Victory Avenue","walk it slow under the pennants."],["The Stage","climb the steps and take the microphone."],["The Tower","the boombox on the roof; you feel it in the pavement."]]},"The Velvet Rooftops":{"arriving":"Beside the Pool, on the tallest roof of a drowned city, late at night. The water came up long ago; the island is the tops of towers standing out of a dark, still sea, and all round it more towers rise from the water with their windows still lit in bands, a red light blinking on each. The pool glows turquoise. Behind the bar, VELVET in tall pink neon.","vibe":"Late and lush: deep red, velvet purple, gold and night blue, nothing in a hurry. The Pool roof has loungers, a bar, sofas round a fire bowl, a small DJ booth, palms in pots and a telescope trained on the drowned city. From it, walks of lit glass with a row of small lights down each side run out to every other roof. Out on the roofs: wooden water towers, skylights glowing from below, banks of air-conditioners, antenna masts, stair huts with a lamp over the door, roof gardens, neon billboards (SLOW DOWN, LATE CHECK-OUT, STAY), a helipad.","happenings":"The pool shimmers; the neon breathes slowly and lifts when anybody plays; the red lights blink on every mast and drowned tower. \"Umbrella\" makes it rain on the whole city. \"Diamonds\" lets diamonds of light fall slowly out of the sky. \"SOS\" makes every red light in the city blink three short, three long, three short. \"Blinding Lights\" turns the night white and red. \"Pink + White\" turns the sky pink. \"Good Days\" brings up a soft dawn colour. \"Kill Bill\" turns the city red. \"I Believe I Can Fly\" sends white feathers up.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Pool roof is the middle and every glass walk to an artist's roof branches from it.","places":[["The Pool","stand in it; lie on a lounger; take a drink."],["The bar","three stools, a barman, the sign."],["The fire bowl","three sofas round it."],["The decks","play the next one, slowly."],["The telescope","look down into the drowned city."]]},"Boom Bap Harbour":{"arriving":"The top of the Block on a hot summer afternoon, your back to a brick wall with the island's name sprayed across it. A boombox on a milk crate, a hot-dog cart under a yellow umbrella, an old-timer on the next crate along. In front of you a street of brownstones with stoops runs downhill to the harbour, and half way down a train rumbles over it on rusted green iron.","vibe":"A block party, and a hard place too. Asphalt, sidewalks, brownstones in five browns with tall windows and stone lintels, fire escapes, air conditioners, people on the steps. Under the El the street is shut with police-blue sawhorses: a DJ on a flatbed between speaker stacks, bunting, a hydrant throwing an arc of water across the street, a kid breaking on cardboard, an ice-cream truck. At the bottom the Block runs out onto a scrap of beach: a pier with a man fishing off the end, a lifeguard chair, towels, stacked shipping containers, a yellow crane. Out in the vacant lots: chain-link, graffiti walls, cars on blocks, tyre piles, hoops. The Boroughs' one sunny island.","happenings":"The train goes over the Block every minute; the hydrant sprays; the boombox plays. \"C.R.E.A.M.\" brings dollar bills down over the Block, and any Wu-Tang song brings out the killer bees. \"Empire State of Mind\" turns the afternoon to evening and lights every window on the island. \"Juicy\" sends gold sparkles up at Biggie's stoop. \"Ms. Jackson\" brings a summer shower. \"Hey Ya!\" flashes the whole block like a camera. \"B.O.B.\" strobes the lights. \"Sabotage\" spins the patrol car's lights. \"The Message\" drains the colour to concrete.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Block is the spine from the wall to the water, the El crosses it, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The top of the Block","press play on the boombox, sit on a crate, take a hot dog."],["The Block","sit on any stoop."],["The block party","under the El. Scratch on the decks, stand in the hydrant, take an ice pop."],["The El","the Harbour Line, a silver train covered in graffiti, and a little station."],["The harbour","the pier, cold sodas in a cooler, a towel to sit on, the containers and the crane."]]},"The Lo-Fi Hills":{"arriving":"The Corner, on a rainy blue dusk. A late-night shop with its sign lit, two vending machines glowing beside it and a cat asleep on top of one; a bus shelter with a bench, and the night bus waiting at the kerb with its lights on. Puddles hold the lamplight. Up the hill, at the top of Lamp Lane, a tower with one enormous lit window.","vibe":"For studying, for feeling safe, for being a little sad at two in the morning. It rains, steadily, always, on soft green hills gone blue in the dusk. Small houses each have one warm window with a curtain half drawn, a porch lamp and smoke from the chimney. Power lines run from pole to pole; vending machines glow at the roadside; round trees drip. There are cats on everything: windowsills, benches, porches, machines. Most of them can be stroked. Nobody is in a hurry and there is nowhere to be.","happenings":"Rain; low cloud; windows that flicker now and then like a television left on; vending machines humming; chimney smoke and steam off the mugs. \"waon to ame\" brings the rain harder and lights the chimes. \"Underwater\" and \"Aquatic Ambience\" turn everything deep teal and send bubbles up. \"Darkest (Dim)\" turns the lights low. \"Awakening of the Flowers\" and \"Flowers\" stop the rain, warm the light and send petals over. \"Getsomerest/sleepwell\" brings the night down and the windows go dim. \"Cup of Joe\" is amber. \"Genesis\" is a pink first light. \"Big Blue\" pulses blue.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Corner, Lamp Lane and the Study are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Corner","go into the shop for a warm can of coffee, a bun and the yellow umbrella; sit in the bus shelter and listen to the roof."],["Lamp Lane","lamps, puddles, houses with one lit window."],["The Study","go in, sit at the desk under the lamp, look out of the open window at the rain; press play on the tape; take the headphones."],["The cats","stroke them."]]},"The Neo Soul Quarter":{"arriving":"The Courtyard, on a candlelit evening. A tiled court under strings of honey-coloured lights, and in one corner a monstera the size of a house, still in its pot. A book and record stall, a circle of floor cushions round a low table of candles, tea and incense. The sky is plum; every window round the court is lit.","vibe":"Slow, warm, intimate. The houseplants took the quarter over years ago and nobody minded: monsteras lean over the rooftops, ferns arch across the lanes, snake plants stand as tall as lamp posts, fig trees grow out of pots on every corner. The houses are terracotta, clay and honey, with balconies full of leaves and candles on every step. Honey Street runs out of the Courtyard under lights strung from pole to pole. Sofas stand out in the open with a lantern beside them. Fireflies. Nobody is in a hurry.","happenings":"Candles flicker; the strings of lights breathe one after another; incense smoke climbs; fireflies drift; the wheel turns. \"Blue Lights\" pulses the whole quarter blue, windows and all. \"Golden\" turns everything gold. \"Red Room\" turns it red. \"Wildfires\" flickers orange and sends embers up from the cube. \"Pretty Wings\" brings white feathers down. \"Ascension\" sends lights rising out of the streets. \"Cranes in the Sky\" sets paper cranes drifting up. \"Untitled (How Does It Feel)\" takes the quarter dark but for the one spotlight. \"On & On\" turns the wheel faster.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Courtyard and Honey Street are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Courtyard","take a book, flip through the record crates, sit on a cushion, have tea."],["Honey Street","the lit windows and the lights overhead."],["The Listening Room","drop the needle; take a sofa."],["The stage","play a slow chord."],["The wilds","sofas and lanterns among the giant plants."]]},"The Roots Bay":{"arriving":"The Yard, on a bright morning just after rain. A great spreading tree with a bench all the way round its trunk, a standpipe, washing on a line, a dominoes table, a fruit stall under a red, gold and green awning. The hills are wet and very green with mist still lying in them, the red-earth road is full of puddles the colour of the sky, and over the bay stands a rainbow.","vibe":"Easy and uplifting, and proud. Bay Road runs from the Yard down to the beach between board houses painted red, gold, green, blue and pink, each on short stilts with a zinc roof and a little verandah, flags on poles, people leaning on the rails saying good morning. Palms along the shore; shade trees, banana and hibiscus on the hills; hammocks; goats. At the bottom the sand: painted canoes, a net drying, fishing huts on stilts over the water. And the Sound System.","happenings":"Mist drifts and burns off; white clouds move away; butterflies; the Sound System's cones beat slowly like a heart. Bob Marley's songs make the cones beat hard. \"No Woman, No Cry\" makes the rainbow blaze and turns the morning gold. \"Ghost Town\" drains the colour from the whole island and brings the mist down thick. \"Pressure Drop\" brings the rain back for a while. \"Chase the Devil\" flickers red in the mist. \"Night Nurse\" turns the light dusk-blue. \"Legalize It\" lays a green haze. \"Israelites\" is a hard gold sunrise.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Yard, Bay Road and the beach are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Yard","sit under the tree, slam a domino, take a mango or a jelly coconut."],["Bay Road","the painted houses and the puddles."],["The beach","sit in a canoe, walk the plank out to the fishing hut, kick the football."],["The Sound System","drop the needle; sit by the fire."],["The dance hall","play the off-beat."]]},"The Isle of Memes & Musicals":{"arriving":"The Homepage, in a pastel dream of the old internet. A plaza of checkered pink and violet marble under a giant glossy orb that hovers and turns; a pink-haired marble bust on a tall plinth in the middle of an aqua fountain throwing up bubbles; a cursor as tall as a house; a hit counter that says you are visitor 000001; and over the way out, a loading bar stuck at 99%.","vibe":"Silly, strange and a bit nostalgic: everything you ever laughed at with your friends, in one place, the way the internet looked in a dream. A sky going from pink to teal with soft pink clouds; soft green desktop-wallpaper hills; squares of checkered marble out in the grass; marble busts on pedestals, Greek columns standing or broken, palms, glossy bubbles drifting up, dolphins leaping out of glassy pools, old beige computer monitors left in the fields with their screens on, and doors standing on their own in empty meadows with light behind them. The Superhighway, a road of glass, runs from the Homepage past old desktop icons (a folder, a letter, an hourglass, a recycle bin) bobbing in the air, to the Dream Mall: an open atrium ringed with columns under a pink lintel, a fountain, palms in planters, a food court, and a little theatre with red curtains where the musicals are always on tonight.","happenings":"The orb bobs and turns; icons bob along the Superhighway; dolphins leap; bubbles rise from the fountains and drift everywhere; the screens flicker. \"Gangnam Style\" flashes everything pink on the beat. \"Wellerman\" and \"Drunken Sailor\" turn the light stormy sea-blue and get the wind up. \"A Holly Jolly Christmas\" brings snow. \"Axel F\" flashes green. \"The HampsterDance Song\" sets the colours racing. Kahoot!'s lobby music cycles blue and purple. \"Fabulous\" is pink glitter. \"Everybody's Free (To Wear Sunscreen)\" is bright sun. \"Bill Nye The Science Guy\" glows lab-blue. \"Gitchee Gitchee Goo\" brings confetti.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Homepage, the Superhighway and the Dream Mall are the spine, and every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Homepage","click the hit counter; sit by the fountain."],["The Superhighway","walk the glass road past the floating icons."],["The Dream Mall","sing the big number on the theatre stage; take a tray from the food court."],["The fields","go through a door that's standing on its own."]]},"Studio Lot Island":{"arriving":"The Front Gate of a film studio at dusk. A forecourt with a gold star set in the middle, a grand cream arch with STUDIO LOT in lights, a guard booth with its barrier up, a water tower with the studio's star, a row of director's chairs (one says YOU), a clapperboard on a stand to snap, and two searchlights sweeping the sky.","vibe":"The magic of the movies, every film you ever loved standing side by side. Through the arch, Backlot Avenue runs between the sound stages: great barrel-roofed hangars with their numbers painted huge, red ON AIR lights over the doors, sliding doors half open with warm light inside. At the end is the Tank: a pool of sea in front of a curved painted sky, with a pirate ship floating in it, sails up, a plank down to the edge and a wheel to take. Out on the lot: trailers (STAR, MAKEUP, WARDROBE), film lights on tripods, stacked flats, prop piles (crates, a fake rock, a column), potted palms, benches under old studio lamps, and a studio cart driving up and down the Avenue. Pink and orange clouds in a violet dusk sky.","happenings":"The searchlights sweep; the studio cart drives; the ON AIR lights blink; the Tank's water shimmers; the campfire sends up sparks. \"Can You Feel the Love Tonight\" turns the dusk deep blue and the stars come out. \"Be Prepared\" turns everything a sickly green. \"Let It Go\" brings ice blue and snow. \"Under the Sea\" sends bubbles rising all around. \"Circle of Life\" is a golden sunrise. \"Rainbow Connection\" drifts through soft rainbow colours. \"Pirates of the Caribbean\" brings a storm, wind and lightning. \"It's a Small World\" cycles pastel colours. \"Jai Ho\" is saffron with a shower of marigold petals. Every song's own light brightens while it plays.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Front Gate, Backlot Avenue and the Tank are the spine, and every lane to a set branches from them.","places":[["The Front Gate","snap the clapperboard; sit in the director's chairs."],["Backlot Avenue","walk between the sound stages and their ON AIR lights."],["The Tank","walk the plank onto the pirate ship and take the wheel."],["The sets","climb the rock on the savannah; watch the screen at the scoring stage; lie in the hammock; sit round the campfire."]]},"The Isle of Scores":{"arriving":"An open-air amphitheatre at night. You stand on the orchestra floor, a round of dark stone with a gold ring and a compass star set in it; stone tiers rise in a horseshoe all round you, braziers burning at their ends; in front of you a great quarter-dome shell of royal blue with gold ribs and little lights, an orchestra of black chairs and music stands with gold lamps waiting under it, and the conductor's podium in velvet and gold, the baton on its stand.","vibe":"An orchestra under the stars: the feeling of every film score you ever loved swelling up at once. A deep royal-blue night full of stars with shooting stars crossing it; long blue-green grass with fireflies; dark cypresses, marble statues on gold-rimmed plinths, red-and-gold banners, music stands left out on the grass, benches under gold lanterns and the odd black grand piano with its lid up. The Star Road, dark stone set with glowing stars and lit by tall lanterns topped with stars, runs out of the aisle between the tiers all the way to the desert where a starship has landed. A lake with swans gliding round it.","happenings":"Shooting stars cross the sky; the flying saucer bobs and turns; swans glide round the lake; fireflies drift; the braziers send up sparks; the music stands and the shell breathe with the music. \"The Imperial March\" turns everything red, pulsing like a march. \"Star Wars\" makes the stars blaze, with more shooting stars. \"Duel of the Fates\" flickers red and blue. \"This Is Halloween\" goes orange and sickly green with embers drifting up. \"What's This?\" brings snow. \"Ride of the Valkyries\" brings a storm with lightning. \"Swan Lake\" is silver moonlight on the lake. \"L'Estasi Dell'Oro\" is a blaze of gold. \"Let It Grow\" turns green. The X-Files theme pulses the saucer's green. \"Soarin'\" brings a bright windy sky.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Amphitheatre with a ring walk round its tiers, and the Star Road out of its aisle, are the spine; every lane to an artist branches from them.","places":[["The Amphitheatre","step up to the podium and conduct the orchestra; take a seat in the orchestra or on the tiers."],["The Star Road","walk the road of stars to the starship."],["The lake of swans","sit on a bench by the water."],["Out on the hills","sit at a grand piano; climb the curly hill in the Halloween town."]]},"The 8-Bit Isle":{"arriving":"The Arcade, at a pixel sunset. A checkered floor of blue tiles; a giant arcade cabinet, taller than a house, with a beating pixel heart on its screen and THE 8-BIT ISLE across its marquee; a row of cabinets down each side with red stools to sit and play; PRESS START blinking in the air (press it); gold coins spinning in a ring.","vibe":"Every game you ever loved, each in its own little world, and everything made of blocks. A violet sky with an orange horizon and pink pixel clouds; grass in flat steps of green; block trees (green, cherry pink and autumn orange), green pipes, rows of brick and gold blocks floating in the air, arcs of spinning coins, red mushrooms with white spots, stepped block hills, benches under pixel lamps and the odd lone arcade cabinet. The Level Road, brick with gold blocks floating over it and pipes beside it, runs to a flagpole you can grab, and beyond it the Ring: a vast silver ring rising out of the ground into the sky, lit blue inside.","happenings":"Coins spin and bob everywhere; PRESS START blinks; the pixel heart beats; screens flicker; the pixel wall shimmers; chickens hop; the flag slides down when you grab it and creeps back up; the Ring's light breathes. \"MEGALOVANIA\" flashes blue and white on the beat. \"One-Winged Angel\" turns everything red and black. \"Wii Shop Channel\" is calm pastel blue. The farm's seasons bring snow, pink petals, warm gold and falling orange leaves. The Halo theme glows green-gold and the Ring blazes. \"Hunter's Dream\" is a pale silver mist. \"Bob-Omb Battlefield\" is bright sun. \"Living Mice\" is a soft green. \"Sharkatzor\" sets the pixel wall racing.","roads":"Laid out on purpose: the Arcade and the Level Road are the spine, and every lane to a game branches from them.","places":[["The Arcade","press start; sit at a cabinet and play."],["The Level Road","walk under the floating blocks; grab the flag at the end."],["The Ring","stand under it at the end of the road."],["The farm","visit all four seasons at once; ship something in the bin."]]}};
